WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.243
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4508.

2
00:00:01.243 --> 00:00:02.486
Begin Session II.]

3
00:00:02.486 --> 00:00:04.971
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alright, I think that looks good.

4
00:00:04.971 --> 00:00:07.457
Today is Tuesday, August eleventh, 2015.

5
00:00:07.457 --> 00:00:10.357
I'm here again with Ms. Carolyn Rigmaiden-Frank.

6
00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:15.329
This is our second interview together for the Mossville Oral History project,

7
00:00:15.329 --> 00:00:22.786
and we are working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville [Louisiana].

8
00:00:22.786 --> 00:00:29.000
And I just want to thank you so much for talking with us again . . .

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:29.330
<v CAROLYN RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Welcome.

10
00:00:29.330 --> 00:00:31.313
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . and sharing your . . . Yes, thank you.

11
00:00:31.313 --> 00:00:32.965
Your beautiful memories about Mossville.

12
00:00:32.965 --> 00:00:38.583
Last time we talked you shared just memories of Mossville and your family’s role in the community.

13
00:00:38.583 --> 00:00:46.843
Today we want to talk a little bit more about what life was like here, just for the record and so it won’t be forgotten.

14
00:00:46.843 --> 00:00:53.783
But first, I kind of want to pick up on what we talked about voting and your father's role in that.

15
00:00:53.783 --> 00:00:55.765
I feel like that's really important.

16
00:00:55.765 --> 00:01:03.365
You mentioned the importance of voting, especially after . . . and I know it was important after the Voting Rights Act that passed in 1965.

17
00:01:03.365 --> 00:01:06.339
You would have been around twenty-three at that time?

18
00:01:06.339 --> 00:01:07.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[agrees]

19
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.891
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember anything about that?

20
00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:12.782
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, I remember that, the voting.

21
00:01:12.782 --> 00:01:24.827
And my father was very into voting because he felt that that was important, because he felt that was a right that we should honor.

22
00:01:24.827 --> 00:01:30.127
And a lot of people still don't vote, for whatever reason.

23
00:01:30.127 --> 00:01:31.573
I don't know.

24
00:01:31.573 --> 00:01:40.245
But he thought it was so important that he would get into . . . He got into it, I think,

25
00:01:40.245 --> 00:01:50.364
mainly to help the people in the community that didn't understand, or that didn't know exactly what all was going on.

26
00:01:50.364 --> 00:02:00.964
He would take them to register to vote because he wanted to make sure that everybody participated that was able to participate.

27
00:02:00.964 --> 00:02:12.045
So this made it . . . Being important to him as a young person that I was at the time, it became important to me.

28
00:02:12.045 --> 00:02:21.682
So I knew how important it was that when I was able to . . . when I became of age to register,

29
00:02:21.682 --> 00:02:24.573
that I would register to vote.

30
00:02:24.573 --> 00:02:32.764
I've always talked to people that say, “Well my vote don't count,” or, “It doesn't matter anyway.”

31
00:02:32.764 --> 00:02:41.436
I always would try and tell them that you look back at our older generation that couldn't vote,

32
00:02:41.436 --> 00:02:53.000
and so this should make you just want to vote just for that reason because they fought for us to get that right.

33
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:02.709
I just feel that one of my daddy’s reasons . . . that was his reason—that that was handed down to him from his uncle,

34
00:03:02.709 --> 00:03:12.418
and I would think from farther back than that, that how far, ever far back, when they first started wanting to vote.

35
00:03:12.418 --> 00:03:18.596
And so that being handed down, and he handed it down to his children,

36
00:03:18.596 --> 00:03:26.540
so I felt that this is something that I would have . . . I had to do to my children,

37
00:03:26.540 --> 00:03:30.070
and hopefully they will do that to theirs.

38
00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:35.808
But it is a very important thing for this community because, I mean,

39
00:03:35.808 --> 00:03:41.545
we were just a small . . . just really small, more or less a town.

40
00:03:41.545 --> 00:03:45.516
Well, I don't know if we were a town.

41
00:03:45.516 --> 00:03:53.019
Might not been enough people, but . . . It was just because of the close knit of the people,

42
00:03:53.019 --> 00:03:58.315
and you wanting everybody to progress and for the community to progress.

43
00:03:58.315 --> 00:04:04.493
That’s the only way that that would happen if we were able to vote.

44
00:04:04.493 --> 00:04:06.258
And if you're able

45
00:04:06.258 --> 00:04:12.437
<c.p>to vote for the person that's going to help you and help your community,

46
00:04:12.437 --> 00:04:18.615
because everybody that run for an office is not going to help your community.

47
00:04:18.615 --> 00:04:27.000
And so it was very important that we vote in order to get the right person in office.

48
00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:30.027
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you . . . Thank you for that.

49
00:04:30.027 --> 00:04:30.532
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[agrees]

50
00:04:30.532 --> 00:04:38.099
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you have any memories of the voter registration in the schools, or the efforts . . .

51
00:04:38.099 --> 00:04:38.604
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No.

52
00:04:38.604 --> 00:04:40.117
<v ARSENEAULT>. . . in the ‘60s?

53
00:04:40.117 --> 00:04:40.622
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Never.

54
00:04:40.622 --> 00:04:46.676
No, I don't . . . I know we didn't do it in the school.

55
00:04:46.676 --> 00:04:50.207
We didn’t do it in our school.

56
00:04:50.207 --> 00:04:53.234
They would just . . . Like I say,

57
00:04:53.234 --> 00:05:02.315
my daddy and some of the other people would take people to register to vote at the courthouse.

58
00:05:02.315 --> 00:05:07.360
But that was about all that I remember about that.

59
00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.910
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember when that was, when he coordinated those efforts?

60
00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.937
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>It was in the early ‘60s.

61
00:05:15.937 --> 00:05:19.973
I don't really remember what year, but it

62
00:05:19.973 --> 00:05:23.000
<c.p>was in the early ‘60s.

63
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.059
<v ARSENEAULT>How long did he do that?

64
00:05:26.059 --> 00:05:30.137
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, he did that for a long time.

65
00:05:30.137 --> 00:05:35.235
That was just part of the things that he did.

66
00:05:35.235 --> 00:05:46.961
He did that, then when it came voting time, he would see that the people had transportation to get to the voting polls.

67
00:05:46.961 --> 00:06:00.725
So he would . . . The different politicians that they were going to vote for would give them money for cars for people to drive others to the polls.

68
00:06:00.725 --> 00:06:08.373
So he would see that that was done, and that everybody that wanted to drive,

69
00:06:08.373 --> 00:06:17.549
that they would . . . he would get money for them in order for them to be able to drive.

70
00:06:17.549 --> 00:06:24.686
They would pick up the people that wasn't able . . . didn’t have transportation to come.

71
00:06:24.686 --> 00:06:28.765
So it was just things that was ongoing.

72
00:06:28.765 --> 00:06:35.392
It just . . . Even though after the elections and stuff, things would slow down.

73
00:06:35.392 --> 00:06:42.529
But that was just something that always was there, because when people needed something,

74
00:06:42.529 --> 00:06:49.667
or somebody in the community if something happened and they would come to him,

75
00:06:49.667 --> 00:06:59.353
and he would go to the representatives or whoever it was that the community voted for if they won.

76
00:06:59.353 --> 00:07:07.000
He would talk for them, too, if they had gotten in trouble or anything.

77
00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:15.341
So, this is where it come in that what you do to help somebody, then they help you later.

78
00:07:15.341 --> 00:07:17.537
So it was always something.

79
00:07:17.537 --> 00:07:19.293
It just never stopped.

80
00:07:19.293 --> 00:07:21.488
And during the election time,

81
00:07:21.488 --> 00:07:30.707
I mean our phone would always . . . We couldn’t talk on the phone because Daddy was waiting for a call from somebody,

82
00:07:30.707 --> 00:07:37.732
and the phone would ring and it was for me, and I would try and talk.

83
00:07:37.732 --> 00:07:42.122
"Get off that phone!" [laughs] And I'm thinking, oh shoot.

84
00:07:42.122 --> 00:07:47.390
I'd be so glad when this is over because we just stuck.

85
00:07:47.390 --> 00:07:55.293
But I know that he was doing that not just for him, but it was for everybody here.

86
00:07:55.293 --> 00:08:01.439
I think everybody appreciated it because they . . . I could tell by that they were

87
00:08:01.439 --> 00:08:10.659
<c.p>glad to know, because they would call him to see who we voting for sheriff, who we voting for district attorney.

88
00:08:10.659 --> 00:08:19.000
So, they liked what his opinions, whatever he would tell them normally that's who they were voting for.

89
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.912
<v ARSENEAULT>How did he decide?

90
00:08:20.912 --> 00:08:31.426
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I don't know. [Laughs] I know he would talk to all of them and see what . . . I guess what they qualifications were,

91
00:08:31.426 --> 00:08:37.639
and what they were going to do for, I would say the community,

92
00:08:37.639 --> 00:08:43.373
how they were going to help or . . . and if they didn't help,

93
00:08:43.373 --> 00:08:51.976
then if he . . . if that was one they voted for, then they wouldn't vote them the next time.

94
00:08:51.976 --> 00:09:02.490
Because I can remember one that was . . . when all the civil rights and everything was up and this one, our state representative,

95
00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:13.960
one of the ladies from here, rather one of the young girls from here wanted to go to McNeese [State University; Lake Charles, LA].

96
00:09:13.960 --> 00:09:24.474
And so Daddy went to talk to the representative to give them a scholarship, or they would give them money to go.

97
00:09:24.474 --> 00:09:35.466
And he didn't want to give them money to go to McNeese, but he would give it to go to Grambling [State University;

98
00:09:35.466 --> 00:09:43.112
Grambling, LA] or Southern [University; Baton Rouge, LA], which is predominantly black colleges here in Louisiana.

99
00:09:43.112 --> 00:09:47.892
So that ended their voting for him the next time.

100
00:09:47.892 --> 00:09:53.149
He wasn't one of the ones that they would vote for.

101
00:09:53.149 --> 00:10:00.317
And all before, they thought he was a really good representative until that came up.

102
00:10:00.317 --> 00:10:10.831
Sometimes you just aren't sure, but you try and pick the best person that's going to help you and your community out.

103
00:10:10.831 --> 00:10:18.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So during that time, how did the teachings of Martin Luther King affect Mossville?

104
00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:26.412
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, during that time, we were . . . I mean, I done graduated, left school, left here.

105
00:10:26.412 --> 00:10:35.384
So it wasn't . . . I don't remember what they did after that out here in the community.

106
00:10:35.384 --> 00:10:47.161
I just wasn't coming over here that much, and wasn't into to know what all they were doing at that time.

107
00:10:47.161 --> 00:10:51.086
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that time?

108
00:10:51.086 --> 00:10:51.647
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Pardon]?

109
00:10:51.647 --> 00:10:57.255
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about that time in the country?

110
00:10:57.255 --> 00:11:12.957
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I know that it was a time when the blacks was really trying to elevate themselves and they wanted to have the same rights as everybody else.

111
00:11:12.957 --> 00:11:21.929
So it was just that . . . Sometimes you have to . . . Like they demonstrated and marched, and everything.

112
00:11:21.929 --> 00:11:32.584
Sometimes you have to just be aggressive enough to do something other than just sit and talk about it.

113
00:11:32.584 --> 00:11:39.875
So that was . . . I think that was a good thing that they did.

114
00:11:39.875 --> 00:11:46.043
I think the March at Washington, D.C. was a good thing.

115
00:11:46.043 --> 00:11:53.333
And the men, the Million Men March, I think that's a good thing.

116
00:11:53.333 --> 00:12:05.671
Even here in Lake Charles they have the Million Men March, but I don't know how many men is in the organization.

117
00:12:05.671 --> 00:12:09.035
But it's good and it's growing.

118
00:12:09.035 --> 00:12:19.690
So it's times when you have to do other things other than just sit and wait for somebody else.

119
00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:24.737
You have to get involved in these things, too.

120
00:12:24.737 --> 00:12:29.784
But at this time, Daddy had moved to California.

121
00:12:29.784 --> 00:12:32.588
He wasn't out here anymore.

122
00:12:32.588 --> 00:12:41.000
So I don’t really know what all they did or anything after that point.

123
00:12:41.000 --> 00:12:43.712
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you move with him?

124
00:12:43.712 --> 00:12:51.307
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No. He . . . My mother died in ‘65, and he remarried and moved to California.

125
00:12:51.307 --> 00:12:57.275
So, no, I was living in Lake Charles at the time.

126
00:12:57.275 --> 00:13:01.614
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he notice any differences between the communities?

127
00:13:01.614 --> 00:13:08.667
Did he ever make a comparison between California and Mossville or Lake Charles?

128
00:13:08.667 --> 00:13:14.634
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, he was just . . . In California he wasn't really into anything.

129
00:13:14.634 --> 00:13:21.144
He was just still with his church, and the district and things.

130
00:13:21.144 --> 00:13:30.366
But he wasn't into anything in Oakland [California] as far as none of the organizations or anything.

131
00:13:30.366 --> 00:13:41.758
He would come back down here because he was still in the Christ Sanctified Holy Church district that consist of Louisiana,

132
00:13:41.758 --> 00:13:47.183
Texas, and Arkansas, so he was still in that member.

133
00:13:47.183 --> 00:13:52.065
They joined in California when he went up there.

134
00:13:52.065 --> 00:14:04.000
So California was made part of the district, so he would still come here for all the conferences and everything, and . . .

135
00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:07.226
<v ARSENEAULT>So he was still a minister?

136
00:14:07.226 --> 00:14:07.764
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

137
00:14:07.764 --> 00:14:11.528
Oh, he never would give that up.

138
00:14:11.528 --> 00:14:22.821
That was one thing that he had . . . He worked at Firestone Tire and Rubber plant for I think about twenty-five years.

139
00:14:22.821 --> 00:14:34.113
But he had a heart attack in ‘64, and they . . . the plant doctors said that he had too much extra activities,

140
00:14:34.113 --> 00:14:44.330
so they told him that he would either have to give up his churches, or still work with them.

141
00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:47.019
So he gave up Firestone.

142
00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:52.396
He went on and retired and just kept his churches.

143
00:14:52.396 --> 00:14:55.623
So that's one thing that he

144
00:14:55.623 --> 00:14:57.774
<c.p>would never give up.

145
00:14:57.774 --> 00:15:01.000
He never gave that up.

146
00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.619
<v ARSENEAULT>That was his life calling.

147
00:15:03.619 --> 00:15:04.143
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Right.

148
00:15:04.143 --> 00:15:04.667
Yeah.

149
00:15:04.667 --> 00:15:12.000
Because when . . . I know that he was really out in the world before that.

150
00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:15.667
So he fought when God called him.

151
00:15:15.667 --> 00:15:29.286
He didn’t want to go into that ministry or anything, but when you're called, eventually something going to happen that you're going to get into it.

152
00:15:29.286 --> 00:15:35.048
<v ARSENEAULT>You're either going to run and get swallowed by a whale . . .

153
00:15:35.048 --> 00:15:39.762
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Yeah, so he became minister in ’45, 1945.

154
00:15:39.762 --> 00:15:41.333
He was ordained.

155
00:15:41.333 --> 00:15:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>How did he tell that story?

156
00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:53.366
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>The people . . . Like I wasn't even born yet, so I didn't know the wild side of him or the . . .

157
00:15:53.366 --> 00:15:56.008
<v ARSENEAULT>Because you were born in ‘47?

158
00:15:56.008 --> 00:15:56.449
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

159
00:15:56.449 --> 00:16:06.576
So I didn't know the part where he was drinking and all this different stuff, gambling, and had stills and all this stuff.

160
00:16:06.576 --> 00:16:10.099
So I just knew what I was told.

161
00:16:10.099 --> 00:16:10.539
My

162
00:16:10.539 --> 00:16:17.144
<c.p>sister and them, the older ones, would tell us about how he was and stuff.

163
00:16:17.144 --> 00:16:29.473
But I can remember he said that the night that he finally gave up all of that. . . My mother would always go to church and take the kids.

164
00:16:29.473 --> 00:16:39.160
And so this one night he went . . . he would go meet her and walk her home because they didn't have a car.

165
00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.802
So he went and met her.

166
00:16:41.802 --> 00:16:46.206
He was standing outside waiting for the church was over.

167
00:16:46.206 --> 00:16:53.251
He said he was standing outside, and he said something just told him to go in.

168
00:16:53.251 --> 00:16:56.774
And he went in and he joined church.

169
00:16:56.774 --> 00:16:59.416
From there, he became a minister.

170
00:16:59.416 --> 00:17:06.461
So that was the ways that God had been telling him and he wouldn't do it,

171
00:17:06.461 --> 00:17:11.305
he still wanted to do what he . . . everything in the world.

172
00:17:11.305 --> 00:17:17.469
But that one night when he went, and he said he was staying outside.

173
00:17:17.469 --> 00:17:20.551
He had no intention of going in.

174
00:17:20.551 --> 00:17:24.074
But when God say it's time, it's time.

175
00:17:24.074 --> 00:17:26.276
So that was his beginning.

176
00:17:26.276 --> 00:17:28.477
<v ARSENEAULT>When was your dad born?

177
00:17:28.477 --> 00:17:30.679
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>He was born in 1905.

178
00:17:30.679 --> 00:17:31.119
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

179
00:17:31.119 --> 00:17:32.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

180
00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:37.581
<v ARSENEAULT>Did he talk about what it was like growing up?

181
00:17:37.581 --> 00:17:50.977
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No. And that was the thing right now that I hate more than anything is that they didn't really talk about things, bad things.

182
00:17:50.977 --> 00:17:53.209
And my mother either.

183
00:17:53.209 --> 00:18:01.581
She was from Branch, Louisiana, and she never would talk about her family or anything.

184
00:18:01.581 --> 00:18:03.814
I know that she

185
00:18:03.814 --> 00:18:08.279
<c.p>worked . . . She started working when she was young.

186
00:18:08.279 --> 00:18:13.302
I know that she worked for this white family.

187
00:18:13.302 --> 00:18:17.209
When she was thirteen, she lived there.

188
00:18:17.209 --> 00:18:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What did she do?

189
00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:21.387
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Maid, I guess.

190
00:18:21.387 --> 00:18:26.011
I don't know what they . . . But that's all I know.

191
00:18:26.011 --> 00:18:31.097
She worked in the house, so she was in the house.

192
00:18:31.097 --> 00:18:36.183
But she was thirteen, and I think that is so young.

193
00:18:36.183 --> 00:18:41.269
How can you let your child go live with these people?

194
00:18:41.269 --> 00:18:45.892
But that was the way that it was done then.

195
00:18:45.892 --> 00:18:52.366
But that's all but really I don't know anything hardly about my mother's family.

196
00:18:52.366 --> 00:19:03.925
We've gone to ancestry.com to try and find, but it's a lot of things we don't know to get started to try and go up.

197
00:19:03.925 --> 00:19:08.548
Now my granddaughter traced the Rigmaiden family way, way back.

198
00:19:08.548 --> 00:19:13.172
She was able to trace them because I told her,

199
00:19:13.172 --> 00:19:22.419
I said well I thought we were Germans . . . that the Rigmaiden started with German, but she got them in England.

200
00:19:22.419 --> 00:19:24.269
So I don't know.

201
00:19:24.269 --> 00:19:28.430
<v ARSENEAULT>Is there like a white side of the family?

202
00:19:28.430 --> 00:19:28.892
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

203
00:19:28.892 --> 00:19:31.204
Oh, yeah, it was white.

204
00:19:31.204 --> 00:19:31.667
Yeah.

205
00:19:31.667 --> 00:19:37.677
And so I don't know if that's . . . They never talked about that part.

206
00:19:37.677 --> 00:19:45.538
Or if that's why . . . I don't know if that's why my mother never talked about her family.

207
00:19:45.538 --> 00:19:50.161
And right now I hate that because I've lost that.

208
00:19:50.161 --> 00:19:59.409
Even some of the older people here in Mossville, well they've died out, so I even lost talking to them.

209
00:19:59.409 --> 00:20:11.430
Because it was some that I would go talk to, and they would tell me things about the family and stuff, but now they're all gone.

210
00:20:11.430 --> 00:20:18.366
So it’s sort of like . . . it's just what you know and what they've told you.

211
00:20:18.366 --> 00:20:26.688
If you have some of the people who family told them things that that's where our history is.

212
00:20:26.688 --> 00:20:29.000
<v ARSENEAULT>It's in the memories.

213
00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:33.339
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah. of . . . And right now it's the younger people.

214
00:20:33.339 --> 00:20:35.750
I mean, they not young.

215
00:20:35.750 --> 00:20:39.607
I mean, we not young no more [Laughs].

216
00:20:39.607 --> 00:20:46.839
But it's sort of in . . . Say the people that's in their seventies and early eighties,

217
00:20:46.839 --> 00:20:58.411
because all the rest of them done passed on that was able to actually tell you from fact, that knew what was going on.

218
00:20:58.411 --> 00:21:01.304
And it’s bad when that’s lost.

219
00:21:01.304 --> 00:21:11.911
That’s why I was glad that they’re doing this with the Mossville community, saving at least the information, what we do remember.

220
00:21:11.911 --> 00:21:14.804
I think that’s a wonderful thing.

221
00:21:14.804 --> 00:21:22.518
And because I said . . . Well first I said well, I wasn’t going to bother about anything,

222
00:21:22.518 --> 00:21:31.196
and then I thought, well I want to at least let them try to put on about daddy.

223
00:21:31.196 --> 00:21:36.982
I mean, I wanted his information here because, he had worked hard.

224
00:21:36.982 --> 00:21:41.321
They just did so much in getting things here.

225
00:21:41.321 --> 00:21:48.554
Not only the recreation, the swimming pool, the pavilion . . . But Daddy he had done passed,

226
00:21:48.554 --> 00:21:56.750
but the pavilion, but that’s my brother’s name on the pavilion, so I know that that continued.

227
00:21:56.750 --> 00:22:07.357
That was the one thing that Daddy wanted was for my brother to continue to do things like he had done here.

228
00:22:07.357 --> 00:22:12.179
And my brother just wasn’t the same type of person.

229
00:22:12.179 --> 00:22:20.375
So when you’re not that type of person, then you don’t tend to do the same things.

230
00:22:20.375 --> 00:22:27.607
But he were into helping the people, but not as much as my father was.

231
00:22:27.607 --> 00:22:33.393
But so, I just wanted to make sure that that got in.

232
00:22:33.393 --> 00:22:36.286
Not so much me or us.

233
00:22:36.286 --> 00:22:39.179
But I just wanted for them.

234
00:22:39.179 --> 00:22:42.554
<v ARSENEAULT>For what they did for the community.

235
00:22:42.554 --> 00:22:43.036
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Right.

236
00:22:43.036 --> 00:22:44.000
Yeah .

237
00:22:44.000 --> 00:22:50.050
<v ARSENEAULT>But we have to get you, too Your story’s important, too.

238
00:22:50.050 --> 00:22:58.300
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I mean [Laughs] my . . . I was just here until I graduated from high school.

239
00:22:58.300 --> 00:23:02.700
But I’ve always enjoyed . . . I loved living here.

240
00:23:02.700 --> 00:23:08.200
I mean, we could just . . . When we was coming up,

241
00:23:08.200 --> 00:23:18.100
you just weren’t afraid of anything, or anybody breaking into your house, or vandalizing your house or anything.

242
00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:20.300
You just weren’t afraid.

243
00:23:20.300 --> 00:23:26.900
Everything was . . . everybody knew everybody, and if they knew they done something,

244
00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:36.250
everybody was going to know who had done it, because somebody was going to tell; that’s definite.

245
00:23:36.250 --> 00:23:41.750
So it’s just . . . Mossville is just a small . . . it was.

246
00:23:41.750 --> 00:23:44.500
When I was coming up.

247
00:23:44.500 --> 00:23:53.850
I know we got the other subdivisions and things, and that made it . . . more people and everything.

248
00:23:53.850 --> 00:24:06.500
But before all the people came in, we were just a little community, and everybody knew everybody, everybody almost was kin to everybody.

249
00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:13.100
So it’s . . . When you did something or something, you would help everybody.

250
00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:15.850
Everybody would help each other.

251
00:24:15.850 --> 00:24:21.350
Because I know when my father had his heart attack,

252
00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:34.000
our neighbors would send their sons over to cut our grass because at that time it was just me and Momma home.

253
00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:36.071
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did y’all live again?

254
00:24:36.071 --> 00:24:38.143
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Just right down the street.

255
00:24:38.143 --> 00:24:41.043
<v ARSENEAULT>So you lived on Old Spanish Trail?

256
00:24:41.043 --> 00:24:41.457
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

257
00:24:41.457 --> 00:24:43.529
<v ARSENEAULT>Old Spanish Trail and Prater?

258
00:24:43.529 --> 00:24:43.943
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

259
00:24:43.943 --> 00:24:46.843
But no. We had lived closer here.

260
00:24:46.843 --> 00:24:47.671
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, okay.

261
00:24:47.671 --> 00:24:51.400
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Matter of fact, we were about . . . Let me see.

262
00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:58.857
We have the water tower, and then we have the Praters, and the Praters, and then Smith Avenue.

263
00:24:58.857 --> 00:25:02.171
And we lived right next to Smith Avenue.

264
00:25:02.171 --> 00:25:07.143
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of house . . . Was it still . . . is the house still standing?

265
00:25:07.143 --> 00:25:08.800
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, it’s still there.

266
00:25:08.800 --> 00:25:12.943
<v ARSENEAULT>You’ll have to give me the address so I can . . .

267
00:25:12.943 --> 00:25:16.257
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>It needs to be torn down, whatever [Laughs].

268
00:25:16.257 --> 00:25:19.986
But Daddy sold it when he moved to California.

269
00:25:19.986 --> 00:25:23.300
And I'm not sure who has it now.

270
00:25:23.300 --> 00:25:29.100
I thought one of the Montgomerys, but I don't know who owns it now.

271
00:25:29.100 --> 00:25:32.000
But yeah, it's still standing there.

272
00:25:32.000 --> 00:25:34.877
<v ARSENEAULT>What kind of house is it?

273
00:25:34.877 --> 00:25:39.192
You may have told me already, but I just . . .

274
00:25:39.192 --> 00:25:43.027
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>It had siding, a green siding on it.

275
00:25:43.027 --> 00:25:51.178
And matter of fact, it’s right next to my nephew's house, [Kerry?] Rigmaiden and his mother [JoAnn?].

276
00:25:51.178 --> 00:25:52.616
Smith Avenue, they

277
00:25:52.616 --> 00:25:53.575
<c.p>still there.

278
00:25:53.575 --> 00:26:03.644
I don't know if they going to move or when they going to move [Laughs], but they're still there right now.

279
00:26:03.644 --> 00:26:07.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were your neighbors growing up?

280
00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:08.882
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I had . . . Let's see.

281
00:26:08.882 --> 00:26:13.118
Ms. Ava Prater, which was our home ec teacher.

282
00:26:13.118 --> 00:26:19.706
And Mr. Audrey Prater, he was married to my father's sister at one time.

283
00:26:19.706 --> 00:26:21.588
Lived right next door.

284
00:26:21.588 --> 00:26:27.706
And then it was the Hartmans and then Uncle Josh, the other Rigmaidens.

285
00:26:27.706 --> 00:26:33.353
And then across the street, we had the Dellafosses and the Paynes.

286
00:26:33.353 --> 00:26:39.000
Not the Paynes right here, but another set of Paynes further down.

287
00:26:39.000 --> 00:26:40.412
They were kin.

288
00:26:40.412 --> 00:26:42.294
<v ARSENEAULT>So not Ms. Vera?

289
00:26:42.294 --> 00:26:42.765
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Right.

290
00:26:42.765 --> 00:26:43.706
Not her.

291
00:26:43.706 --> 00:26:45.118
<v ARSENEAULT>But the other . . .

292
00:26:45.118 --> 00:26:45.588
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

293
00:26:45.588 --> 00:26:47.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Their kin.

294
00:26:47.000 --> 00:26:47.548
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

295
00:26:47.548 --> 00:26:50.286
It was Vera’s father-in-law's brother.

296
00:26:50.286 --> 00:26:50.833
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

297
00:26:50.833 --> 00:26:51.381
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

298
00:26:51.381 --> 00:26:55.214
That lived across the street from us.

299
00:26:55.214 --> 00:26:57.405
And then the Vincents.

300
00:26:57.405 --> 00:27:03.429
So then it was a lot of vacant lots and things.

301
00:27:03.429 --> 00:27:12.738
And it just seemed like that didn't even bother us, that is was so much woods [Laughs].

302
00:27:12.738 --> 00:27:16.571
It was just . . . it was just good.

303
00:27:16.571 --> 00:27:19.310
We walked to the store.

304
00:27:19.310 --> 00:27:24.786
We had the store right here, just across the track.

305
00:27:24.786 --> 00:27:30.262
My aunt and them had the store and the Garretts.

306
00:27:30.262 --> 00:27:31.905
<v ARSENEAULT>Was he white?

307
00:27:31.905 --> 00:27:33.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No.

308
00:27:33.000 --> 00:27:36.641
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there ever a white shopkeeper in Mossville?

309
00:27:36.641 --> 00:27:37.096
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

310
00:27:37.096 --> 00:27:38.006
<v ARSENEAULT>There was?

311
00:27:38.006 --> 00:27:39.827
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, on Prater Road.

312
00:27:39.827 --> 00:27:42.103
They moved there . . . Let's see.

313
00:27:42.103 --> 00:27:44.378
When did they move there?

314
00:27:44.378 --> 00:27:46.654
Probably about in the ‘50s.

315
00:27:46.654 --> 00:27:50.295
I think they moved there in the 50s.

316
00:27:50.295 --> 00:27:54.391
They were the only whites here [Laughs] around here.

317
00:27:54.391 --> 00:27:56.212
It was nobody else.

318
00:27:56.212 --> 00:27:57.122
<v ARSENEAULT>That's unusual.

319
00:27:57.122 --> 00:27:58.032
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I know.

320
00:27:58.032 --> 00:28:04.859
I don't know . . . I don't even know how they came to open a store there.

321
00:28:04.859 --> 00:28:06.224
But they did.

322
00:28:06.224 --> 00:28:15.327
But Daddy still wanted us to go to our auntie's even though their store was bigger and had more things.

323
00:28:15.327 --> 00:28:34.442
But we would go to Westlake once a week and do our grocery shopping and we would just go there over to Aunt [Marah's?] store just to get bread or milk or something in the week that you might run out of.

324
00:28:34.442 --> 00:28:44.000
But we always went to Westlake or over in Lake Charles to George Theriot’s and do all the grocery shopping.

325
00:28:44.000 --> 00:28:46.774
<v ARSENEAULT>When did those stores close?

326
00:28:46.774 --> 00:28:48.993
The ones in Mossville?

327
00:28:48.993 --> 00:28:50.657
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Let me see.

328
00:28:50.657 --> 00:29:01.752
Now, I don't know when the one where the white ones did, because it was still open when I left.

329
00:29:01.752 --> 00:29:15.066
In ‘65, it was still there, but [Aunt Marah?] and them, theirs, theirs must have closed down around in the late ‘50s, I think.

330
00:29:15.066 --> 00:29:21.723
<v ARSENEAULT>Do you remember any like protesting in the ‘50s and the ‘60s?

331
00:29:21.723 --> 00:29:26.161
The civil rights protests or anything like that?

332
00:29:26.161 --> 00:29:32.818
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I don't know of any out here, or even in Lake Charles.

333
00:29:32.818 --> 00:29:38.365
I know that some people tried to eat at Woolworth's,

334
00:29:38.365 --> 00:29:48.350
but as far as for anything big or it being a big problem, it never was out here.

335
00:29:48.350 --> 00:29:52.234
Never did have that type of problem.

336
00:29:52.234 --> 00:29:53.343
<v ARSENEAULT>So school.

337
00:29:53.343 --> 00:30:00.000
So you finished school here, you went to Mossville High School.

338
00:30:00.000 --> 00:30:00.300
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[agrees]

339
00:30:00.300 --> 00:30:03.000
<v ARSENEAULT>And then, where did you go to college?

340
00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:07.273
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I didn't go to college [Laughs]

341
00:30:07.273 --> 00:30:07.883
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

342
00:30:07.883 --> 00:30:10.935
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, I got married and . . .

343
00:30:10.935 --> 00:30:14.597
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you meet your husband here?

344
00:30:14.597 --> 00:30:15.208
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

345
00:30:15.208 --> 00:30:21.312
Matter of fact, I met him at a school event.

346
00:30:21.312 --> 00:30:24.364
He lived in Lake Charles.

347
00:30:24.364 --> 00:30:31.688
And so they would . . . We had what they called the Del Sprites.

348
00:30:31.688 --> 00:30:37.182
It was the high school ladies, the sorority Deltas.

349
00:30:37.182 --> 00:30:45.727
And so they had high school kids that was going to join the sorority.

350
00:30:45.727 --> 00:30:48.779
<v ARSENEAULT>So sprites . . . like they're little?

351
00:30:48.779 --> 00:30:50.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

352
00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:50.824
<v ARSENEAULT>I see.

353
00:30:50.824 --> 00:30:56.176
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>So I was in that, and we would have . . . it was having a

354
00:30:56.176 --> 00:30:56.588
<c.p>coronation.

355
00:30:56.588 --> 00:31:06.882
So we had to go over to Lake Charles to practice and everything, and that's where I met my husband. [Laughs] It was at W.O.

356
00:31:06.882 --> 00:31:15.941
Boston High School, and we were in the auditorium and a lot of these guys, when they'd see a lot of girls,

357
00:31:15.941 --> 00:31:22.529
they hang around and to talk to see who they wanted to talk to and stuff.

358
00:31:22.529 --> 00:31:27.882
And after we had finished our practice and everything, there was a canteen.

359
00:31:27.882 --> 00:31:31.588
We used to call them canteens, on Shattuck Street.

360
00:31:31.588 --> 00:31:35.294
Not too far, about a block from the school.

361
00:31:35.294 --> 00:31:40.647
We all met up there until our ride would come to get us.

362
00:31:40.647 --> 00:31:43.118
And that’s when we started talking.

363
00:31:43.118 --> 00:31:43.529
Yeah.

364
00:31:43.529 --> 00:31:45.588
Ended up marrying him. [Laughs]

365
00:31:45.588 --> 00:31:49.294
<v ARSENEAULT>How long did you . . . Did you call it dating?

366
00:31:49.294 --> 00:31:50.941
Or was it courting?

367
00:31:50.941 --> 00:31:53.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>We called it dating.

368
00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:57.418
<v ARSENEAULT>How long did you date before you got married?

369
00:31:57.418 --> 00:31:58.891
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Let me see.

370
00:31:58.891 --> 00:32:02.327
We dated a little over a year.

371
00:32:02.327 --> 00:32:04.782
Yeah, little over a year.

372
00:32:04.782 --> 00:32:11.655
<v ARSENEAULT>And this is just my own curiosity, this is not a Mossville related question.

373
00:32:11.655 --> 00:32:16.073
But how did you know he was the one?

374
00:32:16.073 --> 00:32:16.564
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well . . .

375
00:32:16.564 --> 00:32:20.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I'm always curious to know that.

376
00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:22.667
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Well, it was his attitude.

377
00:32:22.667 --> 00:32:25.333
He was nice, he was outgoing.

378
00:32:25.333 --> 00:32:28.444
Just had his own opinion about things.

379
00:32:28.444 --> 00:32:31.556
He was just really a nice person.

380
00:32:31.556 --> 00:32:40.000
He told me when he first saw me, he knew that I was going to be his wife [Laughs].

381
00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:43.556
I thought, oh, I don't know about that!

382
00:32:43.556 --> 00:32:52.000
And when he came to the house and met Momma and Daddy, I mean they thought he was okay.

383
00:32:52.000 --> 00:32:59.111
But they liked the guys more or less that I dated from Westlake . . . closer to home.

384
00:32:59.111 --> 00:33:01.778
Some that they knew the families.

385
00:33:01.778 --> 00:33:05.333
See, they didn't know his family or anything.

386
00:33:05.333 --> 00:33:08.000
So that was a minus [Laughs].

387
00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:15.111
But they got to know him, and they liked him, so that was a plus, too.

388
00:33:15.111 --> 00:33:17.778
<v ARSENEAULT>So this was in the ‘60s?

389
00:33:17.778 --> 00:33:18.222
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

390
00:33:18.222 --> 00:33:23.556
<v ARSENEAULT>What kinds of things were young couples doing together in the ‘60s?

391
00:33:23.556 --> 00:33:30.222
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, really it wasn't much that we could do because he didn't have a car.

392
00:33:30.222 --> 00:33:36.889
And so he had to get a ride to come over here to see me.

393
00:33:36.889 --> 00:33:39.111
And so mostly was it.

394
00:33:39.111 --> 00:33:45.778
We would go to the canteen here, and that was mainly what we would do.

395
00:33:45.778 --> 00:33:53.333
Just sit at home and talk and then just go to whatever they was having over here.

396
00:33:53.333 --> 00:34:03.111
If we . . . If it was during school time and they had a ball game or something, we would go to the games.

397
00:34:03.111 --> 00:34:12.444
But other than that, as far as going out, say to the movies and stuff, they had that in Lake Charles.

398
00:34:12.444 --> 00:34:21.333
But like I say, he didn't have a car, so we just didn't have a way to do those things.

399
00:34:21.333 --> 00:34:28.000
So it was mostly just whatever happened here, out here . . . whatever we did here.

400
00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:30.556
<v ARSENEAULT>Did y'all get married here?

401
00:34:30.556 --> 00:34:31.068
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

402
00:34:31.068 --> 00:34:35.158
Well, we got married in Lake Charles, yeah.

403
00:34:35.158 --> 00:34:42.316
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to talk a little bit about . . . since you're . . . Were you a librarian?

404
00:34:42.316 --> 00:34:42.827
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No.

405
00:34:42.827 --> 00:34:45.895
<v ARSENEAULT>That's your cousin, is a librarian.

406
00:34:45.895 --> 00:34:46.406
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

407
00:34:46.406 --> 00:34:48.451
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you ever work?

408
00:34:48.451 --> 00:34:50.496
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, yeah, I worked.

409
00:34:50.496 --> 00:34:53.564
I worked at Gulf National Bank.

410
00:34:53.564 --> 00:35:02.256
I worked at the . . . well, my main job, really that I worked thirty-one years for Montgomery Ward.

411
00:35:02.256 --> 00:35:12.481
I did their accounting in the office, and when they closed down, they filed bankruptcy and shut down in 2001.

412
00:35:12.481 --> 00:35:22.707
Well then I worked for Lowe's as an administrative manager for seven years, and I just got tired of that.

413
00:35:22.707 --> 00:35:25.774
Didn't care for that too much.

414
00:35:25.774 --> 00:35:31.910
And then I went to the school board in the accounting department.

415
00:35:31.910 --> 00:35:36.000
So I worked there until I retired.

416
00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:49.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I was curious just if you noticed any differences . . . like working, has it changed for women . . . the working environment?

417
00:35:49.000 --> 00:36:02.306
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I didn't . . . The only job that I worked that I guess that would have been more of a man's type job was at Lowe's,

418
00:36:02.306 --> 00:36:11.887
but I was in the office so it wasn't . . . But they wanted you to do . . . drive the forklifts,

419
00:36:11.887 --> 00:36:19.339
do all this stuff [Laughs] and that just wasn't what I wanted to do.

420
00:36:19.339 --> 00:36:29.452
But at that time, when Montgomery Ward's closed, positions in the office and things were sort of tight here.

421
00:36:29.452 --> 00:36:34.774
There weren't many, not in the position that I wanted,

422
00:36:34.774 --> 00:36:47.016
so I took that just because at that time it was just something that I could do that was still in my field.

423
00:36:47.016 --> 00:36:52.871
I worked there seven years, so I stayed quite a while.

424
00:36:52.871 --> 00:37:01.919
And I noticed that the store managers, a lot of them, did not like the women workers.

425
00:37:01.919 --> 00:37:08.306
They . . . That was just . . . I noticed that, and especially in management positions.

426
00:37:08.306 --> 00:37:15.758
It was okay if you was the cashier or something, but not in management.

427
00:37:15.758 --> 00:37:22.145
You could tell how I was treated compared to the other managers.

428
00:37:22.145 --> 00:37:30.661
It wasn't that . . . I don't think it was the color, but it was being a woman . . .

429
00:37:30.661 --> 00:37:31.726
<v ARSENEAULT>Your gender.

430
00:37:31.726 --> 00:37:33.323
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>. . . in that field.

431
00:37:33.323 --> 00:37:39.177
That was . . . I think that was a lot of their problem.

432
00:37:39.177 --> 00:37:49.290
We had a couple of managers that was prejudiced, but you could . . . I just had to work around that.

433
00:37:49.290 --> 00:37:57.806
But the main thing I think was that they wanted men in those manager positions [Laughs].

434
00:37:57.806 --> 00:38:01.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Made no sense to me.

435
00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:01.477
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Right.

436
00:38:01.477 --> 00:38:01.954
Yeah.

437
00:38:01.954 --> 00:38:10.538
Because we were doing the job, but you can just tell the feeling because one of the managers,

438
00:38:10.538 --> 00:38:14.831
him and all the guys, all the other managers,

439
00:38:14.831 --> 00:38:17.215
<c.p>would go out and eat.

440
00:38:17.215 --> 00:38:23.892
I was the only one that he wouldn't take. [Laughs.] It was like "Oh!

441
00:38:23.892 --> 00:38:25.800
We forgot about you!"

442
00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:26.754
Oh, okay.

443
00:38:26.754 --> 00:38:39.154
How could you forget about me? [Laughs] And so I just thought oh well, just let that go because they have to come up with something.

444
00:38:39.154 --> 00:38:44.877
So, but it was. . . I knew what it was, but so . . . yeah.

445
00:38:44.877 --> 00:38:45.354
<v ARSENEAULT>Man.

446
00:38:45.354 --> 00:38:50.123
Well, I guess getting back to Mossville a little bit.

447
00:38:50.123 --> 00:38:56.800
What kind of . . . Kind of talking about work and everything and growing up here,

448
00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:03.000
what kind of values do you think your parents instilled in you?

449
00:39:03.000 --> 00:39:07.584
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>They instilled in me religious values, the church.

450
00:39:07.584 --> 00:39:13.314
That we should love one another, do for one another.

451
00:39:13.314 --> 00:39:16.179
They taught me to work.

452
00:39:16.179 --> 00:39:26.493
Even if I wasn't working on a job, but to still be able to work at your home.

453
00:39:26.493 --> 00:39:32.796
My mother was . . . my mother didn't work out of the home.

454
00:39:32.796 --> 00:39:37.380
She was a homemaker; she stayed at home.

455
00:39:37.380 --> 00:39:50.558
And so they told us that . . . The one thing that I said that they didn’t teach me was how hard life can be.

456
00:39:50.558 --> 00:40:01.445
I saw a life that . . . Like I say, my father worked at the plant, so he made good money.

457
00:40:01.445 --> 00:40:08.321
This was the life that I saw—that you work . . . that you have.

458
00:40:08.321 --> 00:40:12.905
I mean, and they lived a good life.

459
00:40:12.905 --> 00:40:16.916
I mean, we lived a good life.

460
00:40:16.916 --> 00:40:22.073
We weren’t rich or anything, but we were comfortable.

461
00:40:22.073 --> 00:40:25.511
So this is what I saw.

462
00:40:25.511 --> 00:40:38.690
I thought that this is what would be . . . when I go, when I get grown and married, you work, and hey, everything's comfortable.

463
00:40:38.690 --> 00:40:44.420
I didn't realize how life itself can be so bad.

464
00:40:44.420 --> 00:40:56.453
As I said, they didn't teach us that when we leave home that you know, you’re out there on your own.

465
00:40:56.453 --> 00:40:59.891
And man, things can get hard.

466
00:40:59.891 --> 00:41:06.766
You can . . . You may not have enough money—how you going to survive?

467
00:41:06.766 --> 00:41:15.361
How you going to still pay your rent, take care of your family, do things?

468
00:41:15.361 --> 00:41:21.091
I wasn't taught how to do any of these things.

469
00:41:21.091 --> 00:41:25.102
Like I say, because everything was comfortable.

470
00:41:25.102 --> 00:41:26.248
And so

471
00:41:26.248 --> 00:41:40.000
<c.p>when I got out, everything was different, because me and my husband weren't making the money that even, say, Daddy did by himself.

472
00:41:40.000 --> 00:41:43.813
So it was tight, and it was hard.

473
00:41:43.813 --> 00:41:49.056
So I always made sure that I taught my children that.

474
00:41:49.056 --> 00:41:56.206
That things is not going to be easy once you get out on your own.

475
00:41:56.206 --> 00:41:59.065
So I just let them know.

476
00:41:59.065 --> 00:42:03.832
At times my husband was like, "Don't tell them that!"

477
00:42:03.832 --> 00:42:05.262
I said, “Yes.

478
00:42:05.262 --> 00:42:15.271
I want them to know,” because this is the thing that I didn’t experience at home, and if I would have,

479
00:42:15.271 --> 00:42:25.280
I would have been more prepared for what was yet to come for me until things got where we were comfortable,

480
00:42:25.280 --> 00:42:28.140
had enough money to do things.

481
00:42:28.140 --> 00:42:36.243
Because it’s hard when you don’t have enough money to do the things you want to do,

482
00:42:36.243 --> 00:42:45.299
or say even do the things your friends are doing, because we used to like to travel on weekends.

483
00:42:45.299 --> 00:42:49.112
We would go to Galveston [Texas], different places.

484
00:42:49.112 --> 00:42:57.692
And then there were times when it came that we didn't have the extra money to do this.

485
00:42:57.692 --> 00:43:00.551
So that makes . . . it makes problems.

486
00:43:00.551 --> 00:43:03.411
It causes marriage problems and . . . everything.

487
00:43:03.411 --> 00:43:13.897
So it was even though we learned the religious part and the things that we should do, how we should carry ourselves,

488
00:43:13.897 --> 00:43:19.617
and do unto others as you would have them do unto you,

489
00:43:19.617 --> 00:43:26.766
and all these different things that we learned, we still did not learn about life.

490
00:43:26.766 --> 00:43:33.439
I think that's the main thing, everybody ought to teach their children about life.

491
00:43:33.439 --> 00:43:42.972
We were like, confined right here in this community because we didn’t really . . . I mean my mother cooked every day,

492
00:43:42.972 --> 00:43:46.308
so we didn't go out and eat.

493
00:43:46.308 --> 00:43:50.598
We didn’t do none of that type of stuff.

494
00:43:50.598 --> 00:44:00.607
I didn't go to movies until I was grown. [Laughs] So it was like we were just . . . the community was it.

495
00:44:00.607 --> 00:44:12.047
So you were comfortable around everybody, because everybody could tell you what, if I was somewhere and I was doing something, I shouldn't do.

496
00:44:12.047 --> 00:44:17.290
Whoever . . . whatever adult was there, they would chastise you right there.

497
00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:21.103
It wasn't nothing waiting until you got home.

498
00:44:21.103 --> 00:44:28.729
And then quite naturally, they would come home [Laughs], and they would tell your parents too.

499
00:44:28.729 --> 00:44:32.542
So you got chastised more than once for

500
00:44:32.542 --> 00:44:33.972
<c.p>what you did.

501
00:44:33.972 --> 00:44:46.364
So it was like we were comfortable here and didn't know anything else, how life is, how life was even in Lake Charles, a bigger city.

502
00:44:46.364 --> 00:44:55.421
So that just shows you how sometimes the small communities, they hinder the children as well as help them,

503
00:44:55.421 --> 00:45:04.000
but because they keep them from a lot of things, and we need to know these things.

504
00:45:04.000 --> 00:45:05.696
<v ARSENEAULT>That's a good point.

505
00:45:05.696 --> 00:45:06.545
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Yeah.

506
00:45:06.545 --> 00:45:10.361
<v ARSENEAULT>So when did you first consider yourself grown up?

507
00:45:10.361 --> 00:45:11.209
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Wow.

508
00:45:11.209 --> 00:45:14.178
I guess when I made eighteen [Laughs].

509
00:45:14.178 --> 00:45:18.419
Me and my brother had got into it one time,

510
00:45:18.419 --> 00:45:25.204
and I told him that . . . Well, my mother had died, and Daddy had moved to California.

511
00:45:25.204 --> 00:45:27.325
We were still both here.

512
00:45:27.325 --> 00:45:30.293
I forgot . . . We got into an argument.

513
00:45:30.293 --> 00:45:34.110
He was saying something, and I said, "I'm grown.

514
00:45:34.110 --> 00:45:42.168
You can't tell me what to do." [Laughs] And then he looked at me like, "I changed your diaper!"

515
00:45:42.168 --> 00:45:51.073
I was like, "So?" [Laughs] I mean, it was like he was grown and I couldn't talk back to him . . . which,

516
00:45:51.073 --> 00:45:55.738
if my parents would have been here, I probably wouldn't have.

517
00:45:55.738 --> 00:46:05.492
But I felt that hey, I can tell them now that I'm grown now, you can’t be telling me what to do anymore.

518
00:46:05.492 --> 00:46:08.461
And it felt good at that time,

519
00:46:08.461 --> 00:46:16.942
but . . . and then when you would think about it . . . I thought about it, I probably shouldn’t have told him that.

520
00:46:16.942 --> 00:46:21.607
He was trying to tell me something, for my own good.

521
00:46:21.607 --> 00:46:25.000
But it was just good to say.

522
00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:28.750
<v ARSENEAULT>Legally, you were grown, so . . .

523
00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:31.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Yeah

524
00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:35.700
<v ARSENEAULT>Who were the people most important to you?

525
00:46:35.700 --> 00:46:40.399
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>My father, my mother and my older sister.

526
00:46:40.399 --> 00:46:42.749
<v ARSENEAULT>What was her name?

527
00:46:42.749 --> 00:46:44.511
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Ida Mae Porter.

528
00:46:44.511 --> 00:46:45.686
<v ARSENEAULT>Ida Mae?

529
00:46:45.686 --> 00:46:46.274
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

530
00:46:46.274 --> 00:46:54.498
She was stayed here until in the early '60s when they moved to Philadelphia,

531
00:46:54.498 --> 00:47:12.709
Pennsylvania because the jobs had sort of dried up here for me and . . . So I had an uncle in Philadelphia and they went . . . they moved there and got jobs over there.

532
00:47:12.709 --> 00:47:23.870
So she . . . When Momma died, she was always like a second mother, I guess you would say, to me.

533
00:47:23.870 --> 00:47:29.744
She was the oldest girl, I was the youngest child.

534
00:47:29.744 --> 00:47:37.381
And it's like thirteen years between us, but we was still real close.

535
00:47:37.381 --> 00:47:39.731
She died this March.

536
00:47:39.731 --> 00:47:42.081
She had been sick.

537
00:47:42.081 --> 00:47:51.480
We had been called before that the doctors didn't think she was going to make it.

538
00:47:51.480 --> 00:47:59.704
So we had already been called, had to go there spur of the moment.

539
00:47:59.704 --> 00:48:07.341
But she always . . . Even when Momma and them lived, when they lived here,

540
00:48:07.341 --> 00:48:14.978
it was a lot of things that Momma and Daddy didn't go to.

541
00:48:14.978 --> 00:48:24.964
Like they didn't go to the ball games and stuff, and she would take me to things.

542
00:48:24.964 --> 00:48:32.013
So she was like the person that [was there for her] . . . well,

543
00:48:32.013 --> 00:48:42.000
I was born during my parents . . . my mother's change of life, so they had gotten old.

544
00:48:42.000 --> 00:48:43.854
<v ARSENEAULT>They were too tired.

545
00:48:43.854 --> 00:48:44.780
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah [Laughs].

546
00:48:44.780 --> 00:48:50.805
So they didn't do things with me like they did with the others.

547
00:48:50.805 --> 00:48:59.146
So it was a lot of things that she did with me, took me places and did things.

548
00:48:59.146 --> 00:49:05.171
So I really appreciated her and loved her for all that she did.

549
00:49:05.171 --> 00:49:12.585
And Momma and Daddy, I just . . . I feel they were the best parents in the world.

550
00:49:12.585 --> 00:49:20.463
I know everybody thinks that . . . I mean most people think that about their parent, but I do.

551
00:49:20.463 --> 00:49:29.732
I just thank God that . . . for them, and for my sister, and for my other sisters, too, and my brother.

552
00:49:29.732 --> 00:49:35.293
But it was just more of a closeness with my oldest sister.

553
00:49:35.293 --> 00:49:39.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What else do you remember about her?

554
00:49:39.000 --> 00:49:42.709
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, like I would go and visit her.

555
00:49:42.709 --> 00:49:46.417
But she would [visit often] . . . She loved Mossville.

556
00:49:46.417 --> 00:49:49.199
I mean, even more than me.

557
00:49:49.199 --> 00:49:53.371
She was . . . She would . . . Their vacations would be here.

558
00:49:53.371 --> 00:49:57.543
Every summer, her and her kids would come here.

559
00:49:57.543 --> 00:50:03.106
They wouldn't . . . I mean they might would go to other places sometimes.

560
00:50:03.106 --> 00:50:07.278
But she felt that this is where she belonged.

561
00:50:07.278 --> 00:50:13.305
And she would come here and I would have to take her everywhere.

562
00:50:13.305 --> 00:50:16.086
Because she want to see everybody.

563
00:50:16.086 --> 00:50:18.868
And that's what we would do.

564
00:50:18.868 --> 00:50:23.503
When she would come, she would go visit and visit.

565
00:50:23.503 --> 00:50:25.821
So I really liked that.

566
00:50:25.821 --> 00:50:31.384
I told them, when she died and I spoke at her funeral,

567
00:50:31.384 --> 00:50:38.801
I told them about the year that she came and she stay . . . I said she came,

568
00:50:38.801 --> 00:50:46.219
she bought a one-way ticket [Laughs] I say she came in November, went home in March.

569
00:50:46.219 --> 00:50:54.099
I said she stayed and stayed, I thought, oh, okay now when you going home? [Laughs] But . . .

570
00:50:54.099 --> 00:50:56.881
<v ARSENEAULT>Getting sick of each other, huh?

571
00:50:56.881 --> 00:50:57.344
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

572
00:50:57.344 --> 00:50:58.272
Oh, wow.

573
00:50:58.272 --> 00:51:02.444
And I said my home was like her home.

574
00:51:02.444 --> 00:51:07.543
Like she would call me and tell me, "Okay, well, Kip,

575
00:51:07.543 --> 00:51:13.570
I'm coming June the fifth, and I'll be leaving June the thirtieth . . .” whatever.

576
00:51:13.570 --> 00:51:16.351
She just called and tell me.

577
00:51:16.351 --> 00:51:25.623
Don't say are you going to be doing anything, did you already have something planned for that time, or nothing.

578
00:51:25.623 --> 00:51:30.722
And so her kids told her, "You need to ask her.

579
00:51:30.722 --> 00:51:34.894
You know to ask Kip if you can come."

580
00:51:34.894 --> 00:51:38.139
And she said, "Well yeah, I do."

581
00:51:38.139 --> 00:51:45.093
And so she told me, she say, "They telling me I need to ask you."

582
00:51:45.093 --> 00:51:48.338
And I said, "Well, you do!" [Laughs].

583
00:51:48.338 --> 00:51:53.901
But she never did, so that was . . . yeah, she was a character.

584
00:51:53.901 --> 00:51:54.364
Yeah.

585
00:51:54.364 --> 00:51:56.219
I really miss her.

586
00:51:56.219 --> 00:51:59.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was she kind of bossy?

587
00:51:59.000 --> 00:52:00.663
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes, definitely [Laughs].

588
00:52:00.663 --> 00:52:07.871
I think she saw me as being like her oldest child or something.

589
00:52:07.871 --> 00:52:10.089
Because . . . let me see.

590
00:52:10.089 --> 00:52:15.634
I think I'm ten years older than her oldest child.

591
00:52:15.634 --> 00:52:23.396
So it's not really . . . Families, you know, it's that length of time between children.

592
00:52:23.396 --> 00:52:31.158
So because she would . . . And sometimes I would have to tell her, “I’m grown.

593
00:52:31.158 --> 00:52:35.040
You can't be telling me these things.”

594
00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:47.792
And then after that, that just goes out the window because we back to her telling me what to do [Laughs] and everything.

595
00:52:47.792 --> 00:53:02.208
So, but. . . I guess I liked it because I did it. [Laughs] It wasn't like I wasn't going to do what she tell me to do.

596
00:53:02.208 --> 00:53:03.317
But, yeah.

597
00:53:03.317 --> 00:53:14.960
She would come here, and we would have to go . . . she would have certain people that she would go and visit,

598
00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:17.733
and sit down and talk.

599
00:53:17.733 --> 00:53:22.723
Now, she remember a lot of the past things.

600
00:53:22.723 --> 00:53:33.257
And I hate that I didn't write . . . we wrote some stuff down, but I just let all that slip.

601
00:53:33.257 --> 00:53:38.248
And now, I hate that . . . that I didn't get

602
00:53:38.248 --> 00:53:43.792
<c.p>all the information like some of the people here remember.

603
00:53:43.792 --> 00:53:46.010
So that's just passed.

604
00:53:46.010 --> 00:53:51.000
<v ARSENEAULT>I think we're digging up some of it.

605
00:53:51.000 --> 00:53:51.496
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

606
00:53:51.496 --> 00:53:58.937
And I'll be able to go back and see and say, "Oh I remember that!

607
00:53:58.937 --> 00:54:05.386
I remember that happened," because I know Edward Julia, she knows a lot.

608
00:54:05.386 --> 00:54:07.370
She remembers a lot,

609
00:54:07.370 --> 00:54:19.276
so . . . I know if she done did her interview or if she didn't so . . . I know I'll get a lot from what she remember.

610
00:54:19.276 --> 00:54:21.260
Her and her family.

611
00:54:21.260 --> 00:54:25.724
<v ARSENEAULT>I think she told us about a sassafras tea?

612
00:54:25.724 --> 00:54:30.685
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, yeah, well I don't . . . I've heard of it [Laughs].

613
00:54:30.685 --> 00:54:34.157
I really don't know anything about it.

614
00:54:34.157 --> 00:54:37.134
But yeah, that's what I say.

615
00:54:37.134 --> 00:54:44.575
I know like her, I know she would remember a lot of the old stuff.

616
00:54:44.575 --> 00:54:47.551
<v ARSENEAULT>I think you're right about that.

617
00:54:47.551 --> 00:54:49.039
I was curious.

618
00:54:49.039 --> 00:54:54.000
Growing up, how were unmarried people viewed in Mossville?

619
00:54:54.000 --> 00:55:02.463
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, they . . . I guess it was . . . I guess it's like anywhere else.

620
00:55:02.463 --> 00:55:04.579
You’re like different.

621
00:55:04.579 --> 00:55:11.632
It’s like people, they'll communicate with you, but very . . . lightly.

622
00:55:11.632 --> 00:55:20.095
It’s not . . . They wouldn't . . . Like you weren't, I guess, in their group.

623
00:55:20.095 --> 00:55:36.316
And they didn’t have a group, but it's like you . . . not unless you were actually their blood family were you like really included.

624
00:55:36.316 --> 00:55:46.895
But if you, say, just . . . knowing you and everything, you weren't . . . you sort of like

625
00:55:46.895 --> 00:55:48.305
<c.p>an outsider.

626
00:55:48.305 --> 00:55:52.537
And that still goes on today.

627
00:55:52.537 --> 00:55:56.768
People still . . . if you're not married,

628
00:55:56.768 --> 00:56:05.937
they just . . . it just seems like you're . . . you don't belong, I guess [Laughs].

629
00:56:05.937 --> 00:56:15.811
Because sometimes I feel like that, because my husband deceased, and I never remarried.

630
00:56:15.811 --> 00:56:30.621
And like some of my friends that’s married, sometimes I don't go around or sometimes I'm reluctant because it seems like,

631
00:56:30.621 --> 00:56:37.674
I guess I just don't belong anymore to that group.

632
00:56:37.674 --> 00:56:44.021
So it's . . . And they don't even think about that.

633
00:56:44.021 --> 00:57:02.358
But it's just the way that you perceive them as thinking because you don't have your mate with you, and a lot of things is couples.

634
00:57:02.358 --> 00:57:04.474
So I mean . . .

635
00:57:04.474 --> 00:57:08.000
<v ARSENEAULT>You’re the fifth wheel.

636
00:57:08.000 --> 00:57:08.618
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

637
00:57:08.618 --> 00:57:15.417
You can take somebody . . . but it’s just still . . . it’s a difference.

638
00:57:15.417 --> 00:57:27.160
Even way back then, I . . . you could know that they talk about different ones that wasn’t married and things.

639
00:57:27.160 --> 00:57:30.868
<v ARSENEAULT>How would they talk about them?

640
00:57:30.868 --> 00:57:45.701
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, [Laughs] some of the things that they would do . . . go out and I guess went and do things that they wouldn’t do . . . Faster.

641
00:57:45.701 --> 00:57:50.028
And it probably wouldn’t even be nothing.

642
00:57:50.028 --> 00:57:53.736
It’s just because they were settled.

643
00:57:53.736 --> 00:57:59.299
When you single, you can do what you want.

644
00:57:59.299 --> 00:58:06.715
Most of the people out here were churchgoers; they went to church.

645
00:58:06.715 --> 00:58:14.750
They weren’t in the clubs and things and some of the women were.

646
00:58:14.750 --> 00:58:23.403
And some of the women liked doing that, so they always talk about them.

647
00:58:23.403 --> 00:58:33.292
But that’s just . . . it’s just a stigma that single women carry, no matter where you are.

648
00:58:33.292 --> 00:58:37.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What about the single guys?

649
00:58:37.000 --> 00:58:41.081
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>It wasn’t nothing wrong with them [Laughs].

650
00:58:41.081 --> 00:58:44.579
I mean, they could go wherever.

651
00:58:44.579 --> 00:58:46.911
Nobody talked about them.

652
00:58:46.911 --> 00:58:53.325
Didn’t talk about them being fast or chasing women or nothing.

653
00:58:53.325 --> 00:59:06.151
It was . . . They were just single and it was fine because they would still go . . . It didn’t matter with them like women.

654
00:59:06.151 --> 00:59:14.314
Women tend to not go to things that where there’s more couples and things.

655
00:59:14.314 --> 00:59:17.229
But a guy will go.

656
00:59:17.229 --> 00:59:21.310
I mean, it doesn’t matter with him.

657
00:59:21.310 --> 00:59:23.059
He would go.

658
00:59:23.059 --> 00:59:29.472
They might talk about him being wild and drinking and things,

659
00:59:29.472 --> 00:59:38.801
but it’s not really the same as . . . You could tell how they talk about the women.

660
00:59:38.801 --> 00:59:50.461
They portray the women as being fast and doing things that, I guess, are immoral, whatever . . . however they put it.

661
00:59:50.461 --> 00:59:58.041
But the men, it’s just like they just do this and it’s fine.

662
00:59:58.041 --> 01:00:00.956
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a double standard almost.

663
01:00:00.956 --> 01:00:02.122
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh yeah.

664
01:00:02.122 --> 01:00:02.705
<v ARSENEAULT>Interesting.

665
01:00:02.705 --> 01:00:03.288
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

666
01:00:03.288 --> 01:00:12.033
And I come to find out that that’s everywhere, no matter . . . even in the church.

667
01:00:12.033 --> 01:00:20.196
A single woman with children would be in church and they’ll talk about her.

668
01:00:20.196 --> 01:00:28.358
Especially if she was never married and have children, they definitely talk about her.

669
01:00:28.358 --> 01:00:37.103
A man will come in and I mean . . . of course he don’t have the children,

670
01:00:37.103 --> 01:00:45.849
but they know what type of man he was . . . that he got children all over.

671
01:00:45.849 --> 01:00:49.347
And yet still, it’s a difference.

672
01:00:49.347 --> 01:00:52.845
It’s even how they treat them

673
01:00:52.845 --> 01:00:54.011
<c.p>in church.

674
01:00:54.011 --> 01:00:58.092
So it’s just a problem with society.

675
01:00:58.092 --> 01:00:59.258
<v ARSENEAULT>Very interesting.

676
01:00:59.258 --> 01:01:05.089
What kind of stories do you remember your father, grandfather,

677
01:01:05.089 --> 01:01:15.000
uncles telling you about World War . . . any of the wars, World War I, World War II?

678
01:01:15.000 --> 01:01:21.983
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No. I don’t . . . My grandparents were all deceased when I was young.

679
01:01:21.983 --> 01:01:26.638
I didn’t have . . . really have any . . . my grandparents.

680
01:01:26.638 --> 01:01:35.367
My uncle, Uncle Rosco, he was in the navy, but he didn’t live out here.

681
01:01:35.367 --> 01:01:48.751
After he left home he didn’t come back and live, so it wasn’t like we were close that he would tell me anything.

682
01:01:48.751 --> 01:01:51.079
But other than that,

683
01:01:51.079 --> 01:02:03.299
none of the men from here ever really said anything . . . because it was like cousins here that went into the military,

684
01:02:03.299 --> 01:02:09.119
not anybody that was real, real close in my family.

685
01:02:09.119 --> 01:02:10.864
<v ARSENEAULT>So no stories?

686
01:02:10.864 --> 01:02:11.446
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No.

687
01:02:11.446 --> 01:02:12.028
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright.

688
01:02:12.028 --> 01:02:17.266
So you said that your mom cooked every day.

689
01:02:17.266 --> 01:02:23.085
What were some of the things that she would cook?

690
01:02:23.085 --> 01:02:25.994
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, my father liked steak.

691
01:02:25.994 --> 01:02:38.215
He didn’t eat . . . he didn’t like pork and beans because he had ate some that had spoiled or something one time,

692
01:02:38.215 --> 01:02:41.706
and the same with potato salad.

693
01:02:41.706 --> 01:02:44.034
So that was out.

694
01:02:44.034 --> 01:02:47.525
He liked steak, gravy and rice.

695
01:02:47.525 --> 01:02:51.599
That was our meal like every other

696
01:02:51.599 --> 01:02:52.181
<c.p>day.

697
01:02:52.181 --> 01:02:54.508
It’s what we ate.

698
01:02:54.508 --> 01:03:00.910
If he didn’t . . . Well we would have fried chicken on Sundays.

699
01:03:00.910 --> 01:03:07.893
We would have roasts, fried chicken and . . . salad, but not potato salad.

700
01:03:07.893 --> 01:03:10.802
We would have other salads.

701
01:03:10.802 --> 01:03:15.458
Momma had to . . . well I’m saying had to,

702
01:03:15.458 --> 01:03:23.023
but she would cook . . . And we didn’t eat . . . Daddy didn’t care for leftovers.

703
01:03:23.023 --> 01:03:36.407
She would cook enough for our meals for that day and that’s what we . . . We very seldom . . . we never did really have leftovers.

704
01:03:36.407 --> 01:03:52.701
When he would be gone . . . A lot of times his churches was in Texas and he would go on the Saturday morning and come back the Sunday evening.

705
01:03:52.701 --> 01:04:06.667
So that’s when we got to eat stuff like hamburgers, hotdogs, things like that because other than that, it had to be a meal.

706
01:04:06.667 --> 01:04:11.904
It couldn’t be junk . . . what we call junk now.

707
01:04:11.904 --> 01:04:18.887
If he wasn’t there, then we could . . . that’s mainly what we had.

708
01:04:18.887 --> 01:04:25.288
But if he was there, it had to be . . . well normally,

709
01:04:25.288 --> 01:04:34.017
it was steak, gravy and rice, but we had to have the gravy and rice.

710
01:04:34.017 --> 01:04:41.000
Gravy and rice . . . whatever we had, we had to have that.

711
01:04:41.000 --> 01:04:42.050
<v ARSENEAULT>Lunch and dinner?

712
01:04:42.050 --> 01:04:42.750
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, yeah.

713
01:04:42.750 --> 01:04:44.850
<v ARSENEAULT>I would've gotten sick of that.

714
01:04:44.850 --> 01:04:52.900
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Sometimes, we would have beans at school, then I would go home and Momma had cooked the same beans and I say,

715
01:04:52.900 --> 01:04:56.400
"Oh you just cooked the . . . we got the same thing.

716
01:04:56.400 --> 01:04:59.200
We got to eat this over again.” [Laughs]

717
01:04:59.200 --> 01:05:02.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What was your favorite food growing up?

718
01:05:02.000 --> 01:05:09.148
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I liked fried chicken and corn on the cob. [Laughs] That was . . . that's what I liked.

719
01:05:09.148 --> 01:05:11.381
Well mainly almost anything fried.

720
01:05:11.381 --> 01:05:13.615
I like fried pork chops.

721
01:05:13.615 --> 01:05:21.656
Because when I stopped wanting to . . . got tired of the gravy, the steak, gravy, and rice, and gravy,

722
01:05:21.656 --> 01:05:28.804
and stuff like that . . . the good food, I guess . . . what they'll say now, the healthy food.

723
01:05:28.804 --> 01:05:40.866
I would fry me a pork chop or some chicken or something when they would eat something else . . . because when I didn't want something that they had,

724
01:05:40.866 --> 01:05:44.440
well Momma would fix me something else different.

725
01:05:44.440 --> 01:05:50.694
So then one day, I'll never forget that, because it hurted me so much.

726
01:05:50.694 --> 01:06:00.969
One day she told me I had to eat what she had cooked, and I'm thinking, I got to eat what you cooked?

727
01:06:00.969 --> 01:06:02.756
I don't want this!

728
01:06:02.756 --> 01:06:04.543
I wanted something else.

729
01:06:04.543 --> 01:06:09.904
And she said, "Well you going to have to fix it yourself."

730
01:06:09.904 --> 01:06:14.818
I said, "I'm not . . . " So I sit there and sit there.

731
01:06:14.818 --> 01:06:19.285
Everybody ate, I didn't eat, and I thought, I'm hungry!

732
01:06:19.285 --> 01:06:26.433
She said, "Well, you eat what we have, or else you have to fix your own."

733
01:06:26.433 --> 01:06:32.241
So that's when I started frying chicken and frying pork chops and everything.

734
01:06:32.241 --> 01:06:34.921
Everybody just liked my fried chicken,

735
01:06:34.921 --> 01:06:46.536
so I say I guess it's because I started young with doing it because . . . I'll say because Momma say if I don’t eat what she cooked,

736
01:06:46.536 --> 01:06:48.770
I'm not going to eat.

737
01:06:48.770 --> 01:06:54.577
And especially after . . . I couldn't eat after everybody . . . if the kitchen was clean,

738
01:06:54.577 --> 01:07:01.725
so I had to hurry up and decide what I wanted to eat and eat it.

739
01:07:01.725 --> 01:07:12.000
So I always had different from the rest of them a lot of the times because I just didn’t care for it.

740
01:07:12.000 --> 01:07:14.740
<v ARSENEAULT>So what about like sweets?

741
01:07:14.740 --> 01:07:17.479
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>My mother made blackberry cobbler.

742
01:07:17.479 --> 01:07:19.123
Very good [Laughs].

743
01:07:19.123 --> 01:07:22.411
And she would bake tea cakes.

744
01:07:22.411 --> 01:07:27.890
But her main thing she would do would be cobbler.

745
01:07:27.890 --> 01:07:30.082
She liked to bake.

746
01:07:30.082 --> 01:07:40.493
We would go, me and my sister would go and pick blackberries and bring them for her to make

747
01:07:40.493 --> 01:07:45.425
<c.p>her cobbler and she would make a peach cobbler.

748
01:07:45.425 --> 01:07:49.808
But she baked cakes and everything from scratch.

749
01:07:49.808 --> 01:07:50.356
Everything.

750
01:07:50.356 --> 01:07:54.740
It wasn't no, like we do today [Laughs].

751
01:07:54.740 --> 01:07:56.932
Everything was from scratch.

752
01:07:56.932 --> 01:08:05.151
And on holidays, oh, we would have every kind of cake, every kind of pie.

753
01:08:05.151 --> 01:08:08.986
It would just be so much stuff.

754
01:08:08.986 --> 01:08:13.918
But everybody, the family, everybody would come and eat.

755
01:08:13.918 --> 01:08:17.205
She would do all the cooking.

756
01:08:17.205 --> 01:08:26.521
Everybody would come and eat, so she would have enough to last, and everybody take things home.

757
01:08:26.521 --> 01:08:32.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Does she have a recipe she was famous for?

758
01:08:32.000 --> 01:08:34.786
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, she didn't do no recipes.

759
01:08:34.786 --> 01:08:35.250
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh.

760
01:08:35.250 --> 01:08:37.571
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, it wasn't no recipes.

761
01:08:37.571 --> 01:08:40.821
<v ARSENEAULT>It was all out of her head?

762
01:08:40.821 --> 01:08:41.750
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh yeah.

763
01:08:41.750 --> 01:08:45.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Who taught her how to cook?

764
01:08:45.000 --> 01:08:52.111
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Probably her mother . . . or, well her stepmother probably, because her mother died when she was two.

765
01:08:52.111 --> 01:08:54.333
So, I guess her stepmother.

766
01:08:54.333 --> 01:08:55.222
But yeah,

767
01:08:55.222 --> 01:08:58.333
<c.p>because I wanted . . . I loved her cornbread.

768
01:08:58.333 --> 01:09:04.111
I asked her, I said, "Well, tell me how to make a cornbread."

769
01:09:04.111 --> 01:09:07.667
I wanted to know how to make it.

770
01:09:07.667 --> 01:09:10.333
And she just said . . . I said,

771
01:09:10.333 --> 01:09:18.778
“Well I don't know how much!" [Laughs] Because I took home ec at school, so I'm used to measuring.

772
01:09:18.778 --> 01:09:21.889
And she said, “Well, I don’t know.

773
01:09:21.889 --> 01:09:26.333
I just . . . put this and that and make my cornbread."

774
01:09:26.333 --> 01:09:40.111
So I had to sit down one day and just look at her and try and figure out about how much she put to come up with how her cornbread was.

775
01:09:40.111 --> 01:09:49.000
But mine never did come out as good as hers, but she started letting me try to make it.

776
01:09:49.000 --> 01:09:54.367
But yeah . . . no she didn't have . . . it was no recipes or nothing.

777
01:09:54.367 --> 01:09:55.262
No [Laughs].

778
01:09:55.262 --> 01:09:58.392
We didn’t get no recipes or anything.

779
01:09:58.392 --> 01:10:02.865
And now my oldest sister, she made stuff from scratch.

780
01:10:02.865 --> 01:10:06.443
Her cakes and everything was made from scratch.

781
01:10:06.443 --> 01:10:09.127
She didn't have recipes or anything.

782
01:10:09.127 --> 01:10:12.705
So she . . . I guess watched more than me.

783
01:10:12.705 --> 01:10:15.388
I didn't sit there and watch.

784
01:10:15.388 --> 01:10:23.439
But she watched more than . . . And so she learned how to do all those things, and I didn't.

785
01:10:23.439 --> 01:10:35.068
I know my daddy used to tell Momma that, "You need to teach her how to cook [Laughs] because she need to learn how to cook."

786
01:10:35.068 --> 01:10:43.118
So when Momma died, and me and Daddy was home one day, and so he wanted some okra.

787
01:10:43.118 --> 01:10:51.616
And I thought, I don't know how to cook okra! [Laughs] And so my home ec teacher, Ms. Prater,

788
01:10:51.616 --> 01:11:00.114
she lived right down the street, so she had come to visit, see how we were doing and everything.

789
01:11:00.114 --> 01:11:02.350
So she cooked the okra.

790
01:11:02.350 --> 01:11:06.376
She showed me how to do it and everything.

791
01:11:06.376 --> 01:11:13.532
And Daddy say, "I used to tell Alice she need to teach her how to cook.”

792
01:11:13.532 --> 01:11:19.346
But I guess Momma liked her food, and she liked cooking for us.

793
01:11:19.346 --> 01:11:25.608
That's what I guess she felt she was going to do all her life.

794
01:11:25.608 --> 01:11:35.000
She didn't think about that she was going to be gone one day, and somebody still had to do that.

795
01:11:35.000 --> 01:11:38.079
<v ARSENEAULT>But you can make fried chicken.

796
01:11:38.079 --> 01:11:38.593
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

797
01:11:38.593 --> 01:11:41.672
Oh yeah, I could do that.

798
01:11:41.672 --> 01:11:43.212
<v ARSENEAULT>And fried okra.

799
01:11:43.212 --> 01:11:46.804
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Now I know how to do okra.

800
01:11:46.804 --> 01:11:47.831
So, yeah.

801
01:11:47.831 --> 01:11:51.423
And I make good popcorn balls [Laughs].

802
01:11:51.423 --> 01:11:56.556
My children all say, "Why you stopped making popcorn balls?"

803
01:11:56.556 --> 01:11:59.635
I said "Well, I get tired.

804
01:11:59.635 --> 01:12:02.201
I don't feel like it."

805
01:12:02.201 --> 01:12:05.280
But see, I like Steen’s syrup.

806
01:12:05.280 --> 01:12:14.005
And they like this other syrup, and I say, "I have to fix it with Steen’s syrup,

807
01:12:14.005 --> 01:12:20.677
and if I don't have Steen’s syrup, then I'm not making popcorn balls."

808
01:12:20.677 --> 01:12:29.402
So when I go over there, they have pancakes, I say, "Oh I don't have my syrup!"

809
01:12:29.402 --> 01:12:33.508
But they don’t care for the Steen’s syrup.

810
01:12:33.508 --> 01:12:35.048
Different generation [Laughs].

811
01:12:35.048 --> 01:12:43.259
<v ARSENEAULT>So if there's one food that brings back a childhood memory, what would that food be?

812
01:12:43.259 --> 01:12:47.365
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, I guess it would be our holidays.

813
01:12:47.365 --> 01:12:55.063
We would have the turkey, ham, dressings, rice dressing, cornbread dressing, string beans, sweet peas.

814
01:12:55.063 --> 01:13:00.709
Just all of the things, and family would all be together.

815
01:13:00.709 --> 01:13:12.000
I think that's the things that I remember more than just the regular meal is our holidays, how they were spent.

816
01:13:12.000 --> 01:13:14.212
<v ARSENEAULT>Your mom would always cook?

817
01:13:14.212 --> 01:13:14.654
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yes.

818
01:13:14.654 --> 01:13:15.096
Yeah.

819
01:13:15.096 --> 01:13:20.404
And that's the thing, like now you say, "Hey, bring a dish."

820
01:13:20.404 --> 01:13:22.173
Everybody brings a dish.

821
01:13:22.173 --> 01:13:24.385
But no, she never did.

822
01:13:24.385 --> 01:13:26.596
Nobody didn't bring a dish.

823
01:13:26.596 --> 01:13:27.038
Everybody

824
01:13:27.038 --> 01:13:28.808
<c.p>just came and ate.

825
01:13:28.808 --> 01:13:31.019
And that was just her.

826
01:13:31.019 --> 01:13:33.673
<v ARSENEAULT>She cooked the turkey and everything?

827
01:13:33.673 --> 01:13:35.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Right, everything.

828
01:13:35.000 --> 01:13:42.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So did she start like the day before, or a couple of days before?

829
01:13:42.000 --> 01:13:42.500
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

830
01:13:42.500 --> 01:13:44.500
She did it all.

831
01:13:44.500 --> 01:13:48.000
Never wanted nobody else to do it.

832
01:13:48.000 --> 01:13:49.500
<v ARSENEAULT>Bless her heart.

833
01:13:49.500 --> 01:13:50.500
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>[Laughs] Yeah.

834
01:13:50.500 --> 01:14:00.000
Now that I see . . . Because I would do the Thanksgiving dinner for all my children and grandchildren and everything,

835
01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:13.000
and then my daughter and them, they started going to Atlanta [Georgia] to her husband's family at Thanksgiving, and so now my son does the Thanksgiving.

836
01:14:13.000 --> 01:14:15.000
He do the Christmas.

837
01:14:15.000 --> 01:14:28.000
So I have to bring the macaroni and cheese because they like my macaroni and cheese, and whatever else that I want to fix and bring.

838
01:14:28.000 --> 01:14:33.500
But now mostly it’s over at his house that we go.

839
01:14:33.500 --> 01:14:38.500
He does the barbecuing and everything a lot of times.

840
01:14:38.500 --> 01:14:40.500
My son-in-law do, too.

841
01:14:40.500 --> 01:14:42.000
He barbecues, too.

842
01:14:42.000 --> 01:14:47.500
They always want me to tell which is the best [Laughs].

843
01:14:47.500 --> 01:14:49.500
<v ARSENEAULT>It's a hard job

844
01:14:49.500 --> 01:14:50.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah.

845
01:14:50.000 --> 01:15:02.500
I can't tell one, "Well, hey I prefer this one than that," because then they ain't going to want me to come and eat [Laughs].

846
01:15:02.500 --> 01:15:06.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Well do you have a favorite?

847
01:15:06.000 --> 01:15:11.056
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Yeah, but I can't say it because if they listen to this . . . [Laughs]

848
01:15:11.056 --> 01:15:12.611
<v ARSENEAULT>[Laughs] We'll never know.

849
01:15:12.611 --> 01:15:13.389
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No [Laughs].

850
01:15:13.389 --> 01:15:14.167
<v ARSENEAULT>That's funny.

851
01:15:14.167 --> 01:15:18.056
So what's . . . Do you have any favorite memories of holidays?

852
01:15:18.056 --> 01:15:18.833
Or just . . .

853
01:15:18.833 --> 01:15:22.333
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Well, it's mainly with the family getting together, and . . .

854
01:15:22.333 --> 01:15:24.278
<v ARSENEAULT>What was the big holiday?

855
01:15:24.278 --> 01:15:27.000
Was it Christmas, was it Thanksgiving?

856
01:15:27.000 --> 01:15:28.680
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>They both were.

857
01:15:28.680 --> 01:15:29.800
They both.

858
01:15:29.800 --> 01:15:35.960
Now, at Christmas we normally would just come together and eat,

859
01:15:35.960 --> 01:15:48.280
and then they would go to their homes to do things with the kids and things, but we mainly would just eat.

860
01:15:48.280 --> 01:16:01.160
But mostly I guess the Thanksgiving is where we would really stay, and just talk about what's going on, what's happening and different

861
01:16:01.160 --> 01:16:01.720
<c.p>things.

862
01:16:01.720 --> 01:16:08.440
It was really a good time because you looked forward to that.

863
01:16:08.440 --> 01:16:17.400
To you meeting and just doing things with family, which you don't do all the time.

864
01:16:17.400 --> 01:16:28.040
So it was just a joyous time to just sit and talk and just reminisce about things—what you did,

865
01:16:28.040 --> 01:16:32.520
your family done, what all you were doing.

866
01:16:32.520 --> 01:16:35.880
Sometimes now, I think about that.

867
01:16:35.880 --> 01:16:49.880
I hate that the parents, that they're gone and they didn’t get to see all of their grandchildren, or which would be their great grandchildren.

868
01:16:49.880 --> 01:16:58.840
And all the children now that's here, they . . . all they know is what we tell them.

869
01:16:58.840 --> 01:17:08.920
So it's . . . I miss that part with them being there with the ones of us that was there,

870
01:17:08.920 --> 01:17:16.200
and then when they passed and then it was just a missed spot,

871
01:17:16.200 --> 01:17:22.920
yeah it was just . . . Because my mother like held the family together.

872
01:17:22.920 --> 01:17:31.880
At the time, I didn't realize how her role was so important to all of us.

873
01:17:31.880 --> 01:17:38.600
And then when she passed, it's like . . . we didn't get together anymore.

874
01:17:38.600 --> 01:17:51.480
It was like, you on your own, this is on their own, and Daddy was with whoever . . . wherever he would decide to go.

875
01:17:51.480 --> 01:18:15.000
So it lets me know that how important her role was and that . . . I guess she knew that her role was important because things that we would . . . I would always go to her first before I would ask Daddy for anything.

876
01:18:15.000 --> 01:18:19.271
I know that Daddy took care of all the finances.

877
01:18:19.271 --> 01:18:24.823
But if it was something I wanted, I would go and ask her.

878
01:18:24.823 --> 01:18:28.667
And then she would say, "Go ask your daddy."

879
01:18:28.667 --> 01:18:30.802
And I would think, "Oh."

880
01:18:30.802 --> 01:18:33.792
I say, Daddy, he sits up there.

881
01:18:33.792 --> 01:18:37.208
He knows I'm waiting to ask him something.

882
01:18:37.208 --> 01:18:39.344
He would read the paper.

883
01:18:39.344 --> 01:18:42.333
He would hold that paper up there.

884
01:18:42.333 --> 01:18:46.177
And I don't think he ever turned the page.

885
01:18:46.177 --> 01:18:49.594
He would sit up there with that paper.

886
01:18:49.594 --> 01:18:58.990
I would say, "Daddy, I need to know if you would buy me a pair of shoes, if you would do this.”

887
01:18:58.990 --> 01:19:03.260
And he would just sit up there, wouldn't say nothing.

888
01:19:03.260 --> 01:19:10.521
And I'm thinking, now he hear me, but he would just still . . . he was reading the paper.

889
01:19:10.521 --> 01:19:13.938
So, I had to wait until he finished.

890
01:19:13.938 --> 01:19:18.208
And then he would tell me, "Go ask your momma."

891
01:19:18.208 --> 01:19:19.062
I was

892
01:19:19.062 --> 01:19:25.896
<c.p>going back and forth, and I would say, "Oh, Momma, Daddy said for you to decide."

893
01:19:25.896 --> 01:19:31.448
I could always more or less get her to do what I wanted.

894
01:19:31.448 --> 01:19:33.583
She was the easy one.

895
01:19:33.583 --> 01:19:39.562
I guess in her role of just being . . . talking to us and nourishing us,

896
01:19:39.562 --> 01:19:44.260
that when she passed, it's like our family almost fell apart.

897
01:19:44.260 --> 01:19:45.969
We weren't close anymore.

898
01:19:45.969 --> 01:19:51.948
I mean me and my older sister, it's like me and her was close,

899
01:19:51.948 --> 01:19:57.500
and her and my brother was close because they were closer in age,

900
01:19:57.500 --> 01:20:04.760
and . . . but, it's like me and the others . . . I don't know, we just weren't that close anymore.

901
01:20:04.760 --> 01:20:09.031
It's like Momma kept everything together, and everything running smooth.

902
01:20:09.031 --> 01:20:12.448
And Daddy just didn't have that ability [laughs].

903
01:20:12.448 --> 01:20:14.583
He just couldn’t do it.

904
01:20:14.583 --> 01:20:18.000
He tried, but he couldn’t do it.

905
01:20:18.000 --> 01:20:22.238
So it's just really important that . . . family.

906
01:20:22.238 --> 01:20:31.318
And like . . . This is the thing with the community, that now it's just going away.

907
01:20:31.318 --> 01:20:40.399
But the community now, or at least these years, weren't what it was years ago.

908
01:20:40.399 --> 01:20:42.215
It definitely wasn't.

909
01:20:42.215 --> 01:20:45.242
The people didn’t get along.

910
01:20:45.242 --> 01:20:47.664
It's just different things.

911
01:20:47.664 --> 01:20:50.085
It had really changed.

912
01:20:50.085 --> 01:20:57.955
But when I was coming up, it was just a good, beautiful place.

913
01:20:57.955 --> 01:21:06.430
I mean, you just felt home and you always wanted to come back home.

914
01:21:06.430 --> 01:21:15.511
No matter where you were or anything that came up, you wanted to come home.

915
01:21:15.511 --> 01:21:23.381
But once people left, these later ones, well, it just didn't matter anymore.

916
01:21:23.381 --> 01:21:32.462
Because I hated when they closed the school, because I saw where my ancestors' house,

917
01:21:32.462 --> 01:21:42.753
they fought and worked hard for us to get that school, and that they let it close.

918
01:21:42.753 --> 01:21:50.018
I thought that was awful for the people that was left here.

919
01:21:50.018 --> 01:21:56.677
But, that was a different generation, ones that weren't born here.

920
01:21:56.677 --> 01:22:09.996
They had come in, so they didn't have the . . . they didn't see or know how the older people had fought for that.

921
01:22:09.996 --> 01:22:20.892
And you had to be here and see and know that it just wasn't given to this community.

922
01:22:20.892 --> 01:22:33.000
The people had to keep on going and going and with meetings and fighting for us to get this.

923
01:22:33.000 --> 01:22:36.958
When they closed it, I really hated that.

924
01:22:36.958 --> 01:22:44.379
But I wasn't living here anymore, so it wasn't nothing that really I could do.

925
01:22:44.379 --> 01:22:50.811
And the ones that was trying to keep it open, it just wasn't

926
01:22:50.811 --> 01:22:52.789
<c.p>enough to do it.

927
01:22:52.789 --> 01:22:54.768
Then it started dwindling.

928
01:22:54.768 --> 01:22:59.716
The people started sending they kids to Maplewood, to Westlake.

929
01:22:59.716 --> 01:23:04.663
So Mossville, the school just didn't have enough children anymore.

930
01:23:04.663 --> 01:23:15.547
So they knocked it down to a middle school, and then they knocked it down to elementary, and then they closed it.

931
01:23:15.547 --> 01:23:20.495
So for the ones that know what the people did,

932
01:23:20.495 --> 01:23:34.842
how they struggled and fought for us to get all of these things that's here . . . you would had to have been here to know that it wasn't just given.

933
01:23:34.842 --> 01:23:39.789
I mean, they struggled to get these things for us.

934
01:23:39.789 --> 01:23:43.747
So, and for to just give it up.

935
01:23:43.747 --> 01:23:45.232
That was hard.

936
01:23:45.232 --> 01:23:49.684
I clipped out all the, everything in the papers,

937
01:23:49.684 --> 01:24:00.568
all the little clippings of the school every time they would have meetings at the school board about the school and everything.

938
01:24:00.568 --> 01:24:07.989
Even when Sasol bought the school . . . because I was working at the school board office,

939
01:24:07.989 --> 01:24:14.421
and so they had said that Sasol was coming to buy Mossville School.

940
01:24:14.421 --> 01:24:16.895
And I said, "I'm going.”

941
01:24:16.895 --> 01:24:20.853
Because they said, it was like an auction.

942
01:24:20.853 --> 01:24:24.316
It was going to be an auction.

943
01:24:24.316 --> 01:24:28.274
I said, "I'm going to go and see."

944
01:24:28.274 --> 01:24:36.189
So I told my supervisor that I was going to see the buying of the school.

945
01:24:36.189 --> 01:24:40.147
So I went and I sat in there,

946
01:24:40.147 --> 01:24:50.042
and they started the auction with I don't know how many millions [Laughs] but I thought [I could buy it].

947
01:24:50.042 --> 01:24:54.000
But I don't have enough, nearly enough.

948
01:24:54.000 --> 01:24:56.786
But it was just good.

949
01:24:56.786 --> 01:25:08.487
I guess it was a final thing for me with the school to know that it was no longer Mossville School,

950
01:25:08.487 --> 01:25:12.387
that it was going to be Sasol.

951
01:25:12.387 --> 01:25:17.402
And I saw them sign the papers and everything.

952
01:25:17.402 --> 01:25:25.203
But as . . . I don't know how other people feel or felt about this community,

953
01:25:25.203 --> 01:25:31.889
but I know me and my family, I know we loved Mossville.

954
01:25:31.889 --> 01:25:41.919
Even though I left, my other two sisters left . . . Well, we all left really, except for my brother.

955
01:25:41.919 --> 01:25:48.605
He was the only one really that stayed here until he died.

956
01:25:48.605 --> 01:25:58.077
But it was just still a place where, when the parents was here, that we would come.

957
01:25:58.077 --> 01:26:03.092
After our parents passed and Daddy moved to California,

958
01:26:03.092 --> 01:26:13.122
then I would still come to my girlfriends' houses and stuff, but it just wasn't the same anymore.

959
01:26:13.122 --> 01:26:22.037
From just Mossville, if somebody would say Mossville that wasn't brought up here, wasn't raised here.

960
01:26:22.037 --> 01:26:25.937
<c.p>It was just another place to them.

961
01:26:25.937 --> 01:26:28.166
It wasn't anything special.

962
01:26:28.166 --> 01:26:36.524
But if you were raised here, and you knew all the people that was here,

963
01:26:36.524 --> 01:26:41.539
it's like . . . to you, this is like a palace.

964
01:26:41.539 --> 01:26:47.111
Or just, I mean, just . . . it's just a beautiful place.

965
01:26:47.111 --> 01:26:53.240
But you have to have lived it and loved the people.

966
01:26:53.240 --> 01:26:58.255
I know a lot of people that moved in.

967
01:26:58.255 --> 01:27:07.170
Even when they moved it, I know some people moved in in 1957 after Hurricane Audrey.

968
01:27:07.170 --> 01:27:15.528
It tore up some of the people homes and stuff, and so they came here.

969
01:27:15.528 --> 01:27:18.871
And I know even as grown.

970
01:27:18.871 --> 01:27:21.100
Just talking to them.

971
01:27:21.100 --> 01:27:25.000
They didn't like Mossville that much.

972
01:27:25.000 --> 01:27:33.545
So it's a difference in you being brought up here from birth than you moving in later.

973
01:27:33.545 --> 01:27:39.075
You don't think as much of the people, of the place.

974
01:27:39.075 --> 01:27:51.139
It may be some that did, but the majority of them don't because they didn't do the things that we did as little children.

975
01:27:51.139 --> 01:27:52.144
Our playing.

976
01:27:52.144 --> 01:27:58.679
We didn't have a playground to go and play different things like that.

977
01:27:58.679 --> 01:28:01.695
We would play just among ourselves.

978
01:28:01.695 --> 01:28:08.230
We would do things, like we would go crawfishing at the railroad track.

979
01:28:08.230 --> 01:28:13.759
We would make our pole, tie a string on a stick,

980
01:28:13.759 --> 01:28:21.802
and we would get some kind of piece of fat meat, or whatever our parents had.

981
01:28:21.802 --> 01:28:25.321
We would go crawfishing as a group.

982
01:28:25.321 --> 01:28:28.840
We normally did things as a group.

983
01:28:28.840 --> 01:28:34.872
The whole . . . all the little children that was in our little section.

984
01:28:34.872 --> 01:28:45.428
I guess I'll say our little section, because you had the Mossville further down that, they did things in their group.

985
01:28:45.428 --> 01:28:51.460
But when we started school, we were all like one big family.

986
01:28:51.460 --> 01:28:59.000
We knew each other from going to church and different little things like that.

987
01:28:59.000 --> 01:29:07.509
But all the . . . We know we played in our own little sections, and everybody would play.

988
01:29:07.509 --> 01:29:14.422
It wouldn’t be like we wouldn't let this one play or that one.

989
01:29:14.422 --> 01:29:17.613
We all did the same things.

990
01:29:17.613 --> 01:29:24.526
We would . . . We made our own swings, or somebody made them for us.

991
01:29:24.526 --> 01:29:30.908
And so we would play badminton, outdoor games, because we was outside.

992
01:29:30.908 --> 01:29:33.035
We did stuff outside.

993
01:29:33.035 --> 01:29:39.416
We didn't have computers and all this stuff to do things inside.

994
01:29:39.416 --> 01:29:43.139
So we just did things, played softball.

995
01:29:43.139 --> 01:29:46.329
But we all did it together.

996
01:29:46.329 --> 01:29:53.775
It was like we were one big, big family that we did things together.

997
01:29:53.775 --> 01:29:54.306
And

998
01:29:54.306 --> 01:29:59.624
<c.p>when I moved from here, this is what I missed,

999
01:29:59.624 --> 01:30:15.046
is the family atmosphere of everybody being able to tell you what to do if you were a child and they saw you were doing something you shouldn't do.

1000
01:30:15.046 --> 01:30:19.301
That they could tell you what to do.

1001
01:30:19.301 --> 01:30:31.000
And it wouldn't be no nothing that your parents would say, "Oh, no, you can't tell my child what to do."

1002
01:30:31.000 --> 01:30:37.972
Everything was so different when I moved to Lake Charles for me.

1003
01:30:37.972 --> 01:30:41.458
We didn't have that closeness anymore.

1004
01:30:41.458 --> 01:30:43.201
That was gone.

1005
01:30:43.201 --> 01:30:47.849
You were close to whatever family was there.

1006
01:30:47.849 --> 01:30:49.592
Normally that's it.

1007
01:30:49.592 --> 01:30:56.564
You might know your neighbors, and you might not know your neighbors.

1008
01:30:56.564 --> 01:31:07.603
So and then so much goes on in the bigger cities, and so you had to, more or less,

1009
01:31:07.603 --> 01:31:18.642
it wasn’t as bad as it is today, but you had to really watch your children playing and everything.

1010
01:31:18.642 --> 01:31:23.291
Now, unless they would play in a group.

1011
01:31:23.291 --> 01:31:30.844
A lot of times, kids would get together and play in a group.

1012
01:31:30.844 --> 01:31:33.168
They're different from adults.

1013
01:31:33.168 --> 01:31:36.654
Adults mainly stay to themselves more.

1014
01:31:36.654 --> 01:31:42.464
But when . . . Out here, I guess because everybody knew everybody.

1015
01:31:42.464 --> 01:31:45.369
It's just a different atmosphere.

1016
01:31:45.369 --> 01:31:51.179
It's . . . You would have to have lived it, I guess,

1017
01:31:51.179 --> 01:31:59.894
to really understand and know exactly how everything was because if you didn't live it,

1018
01:31:59.894 --> 01:32:09.190
then you wouldn't know how . . . I mean, even say if it was fifty families out here.

1019
01:32:09.190 --> 01:32:15.000
This was like one big family of fifty families.

1020
01:32:15.000 --> 01:32:24.645
If somebody needed help, if somebody was in a financial bind where they may not have a job,

1021
01:32:24.645 --> 01:32:33.755
be working at this time, and need food for their family, they always could go to somebody.

1022
01:32:33.755 --> 01:32:40.185
It wasn't like you . . . I guess they had welfare and all that.

1023
01:32:40.185 --> 01:32:41.257
Food stamps.

1024
01:32:41.257 --> 01:32:42.864
I don't know.

1025
01:32:42.864 --> 01:32:46.615
I guess they had that back then.

1026
01:32:46.615 --> 01:32:48.758
I really don't know.

1027
01:32:48.758 --> 01:32:52.509
Because everybody did their own thing here.

1028
01:32:52.509 --> 01:32:56.260
We very seldom went to Lake Charles.

1029
01:32:56.260 --> 01:33:01.619
We went to visit, I had the cousins over there.

1030
01:33:01.619 --> 01:33:04.834
I remember we went to visit.

1031
01:33:04.834 --> 01:33:11.264
We would go to Sear's every so often if we needed something.

1032
01:33:11.264 --> 01:33:15.015
But other than that, we were home.

1033
01:33:15.015 --> 01:33:16.623
We were here.

1034
01:33:16.623 --> 01:33:32.698
And so it's . . . I know that the people that . . . I'm sure a lot of them that sold their homes and everything . . . and I say that I don't know why the

1035
01:33:32.698 --> 01:33:41.272
<c.p>ones that hasn't sold yet . . . some of them say they're not going to sell and things.

1036
01:33:41.272 --> 01:33:42.879
I wonder why.

1037
01:33:42.879 --> 01:33:49.309
Why would you want to live around all the plants coming up?

1038
01:33:49.309 --> 01:33:56.275
But I guess they just trying to hang on to what it was.

1039
01:33:56.275 --> 01:33:57.883
But that's gone.

1040
01:33:57.883 --> 01:34:08.064
So many people done moved out when I come and see and I say, "Oh, whose house was this?"

1041
01:34:08.064 --> 01:34:10.208
Because it's nothing now.

1042
01:34:10.208 --> 01:34:16.102
I just . . . it just makes me think that . . . after a while,

1043
01:34:16.102 --> 01:34:21.996
next few years, it's not going to be no houses here.

1044
01:34:21.996 --> 01:34:32.177
It's just going to be . . . I guess whatever Sasol put if they going to put something down this part.

1045
01:34:32.177 --> 01:34:37.000
I know they coming down Evergreen Drive now.

1046
01:34:37.000 --> 01:34:44.883
But the thing that now my family and all is buried at the cemetery, Morning Star.

1047
01:34:44.883 --> 01:34:48.824
So I haven't been to the grave lately.

1048
01:34:48.824 --> 01:35:00.155
I don't know . . . I know that I wrote the Informer to inquire whether or not we would still be able to go there,

1049
01:35:00.155 --> 01:35:03.604
to go in the graveyard and everything.

1050
01:35:03.604 --> 01:35:10.501
And they say yes, that it will still be access to go back there.

1051
01:35:10.501 --> 01:35:13.457
But I haven't went to see.

1052
01:35:13.457 --> 01:35:25.282
Somebody had told me that I guess Sasol had did work on the road to go back there, because that's where my family is.

1053
01:35:25.282 --> 01:35:33.657
So that's the thing that if . . . when I come over here now, that's what it would be,

1054
01:35:33.657 --> 01:35:44.496
to go over there and . . . I know one of my cousins used to take care of the cemetery . . . our family and stuff.

1055
01:35:44.496 --> 01:35:54.842
He passed, so I don't even know what it looks like now, if it's . . . how much the grass has grown, whatever.

1056
01:35:54.842 --> 01:36:00.261
I would go out there, me and a friend of mine,

1057
01:36:00.261 --> 01:36:09.622
and we would clean and paint my parents' and my family's graves and things to keep that part up,

1058
01:36:09.622 --> 01:36:17.012
but I hadn't been in years, so I don't even know what . . . how it is.

1059
01:36:17.012 --> 01:36:29.821
Somebody told me they had put a gate up, but I don't know if that's true or not because I hadn't driven back there to see,

1060
01:36:29.821 --> 01:36:42.138
but that would be my main reason for coming out here now because most of the people that's gone that I would come and visit.

1061
01:36:42.138 --> 01:36:44.601
It's a few still here.

1062
01:36:44.601 --> 01:36:47.557
My sister-in-law, nephew, they still here.

1063
01:36:47.557 --> 01:36:55.933
And I have some friends, but other than that I wouldn't be coming to . . . coming out here.

1064
01:36:55.933 --> 01:37:04.308
I don't know even if they going to . . . if it's not going to be a name anymore.

1065
01:37:04.308 --> 01:37:07.757
I don't know what they . . . because it

1066
01:37:07.757 --> 01:37:10.713
<c.p>really won't be a community anymore.

1067
01:37:10.713 --> 01:37:15.639
So I don't even know if it'll still be Mossville.

1068
01:37:15.639 --> 01:37:22.044
I guess, even with the plants, I guess that's how they'll be identified.

1069
01:37:22.044 --> 01:37:23.029
In Mossville.

1070
01:37:23.029 --> 01:37:25.000
I don't know.

1071
01:37:25.000 --> 01:37:34.622
But that's . . . It's just . . . Fifty years ago I wouldn't have never thought that this would happen.

1072
01:37:34.622 --> 01:37:40.636
I wouldn't have never ever thought that this would happen.

1073
01:37:40.636 --> 01:37:48.455
Because for myself, I always thought I would come back and live here.

1074
01:37:48.455 --> 01:37:58.678
But my husband didn't want to live here, and my children didn't really want to live here.

1075
01:37:58.678 --> 01:38:07.699
It was . . . But I always felt that I wanted to come back and live here.

1076
01:38:07.699 --> 01:38:12.510
I really enjoyed my childhood and everything here.

1077
01:38:12.510 --> 01:38:16.720
It's just an ideal place to live.

1078
01:38:16.720 --> 01:38:23.937
But I know things changed when the other subdivisions and things came.

1079
01:38:23.937 --> 01:38:26.944
Crime and different things happen.

1080
01:38:26.944 --> 01:38:29.951
And so it was different.

1081
01:38:29.951 --> 01:38:32.357
It started being different.

1082
01:38:32.357 --> 01:38:44.986
But the Mossville that I knew was a Mossville that I loved, and I loved everything about it . . . the school, everything.

1083
01:38:44.986 --> 01:38:49.196
Even when we had the little buildings.

1084
01:38:49.196 --> 01:38:50.399
The school.

1085
01:38:50.399 --> 01:38:51.000
Everything.

1086
01:38:51.000 --> 01:38:54.608
We were . . . We had good teachers.

1087
01:38:54.608 --> 01:38:56.413
They taught us.

1088
01:38:56.413 --> 01:38:58.818
They were really concerned.

1089
01:38:58.818 --> 01:39:01.825
They wanted us to learn.

1090
01:39:01.825 --> 01:39:05.434
And that is very, very important.

1091
01:39:05.434 --> 01:39:10.846
Still today, if you have a good teacher, oh,

1092
01:39:10.846 --> 01:39:21.070
that is one of the . . . Education is one of the greatest things that a child could get.

1093
01:39:21.070 --> 01:39:33.098
If you have a good teacher, that's really . . . because . . . They weren't concerned not just about say two or three children,

1094
01:39:33.098 --> 01:39:37.909
but they were concerned about all of us.

1095
01:39:37.909 --> 01:39:46.329
And that just really made a difference in a lot of the children lives.

1096
01:39:46.329 --> 01:39:53.545
Not all of them did things after they left school or anything.

1097
01:39:53.545 --> 01:40:04.972
But the majority of us that was here in the beginning, they all majority of them had good lives.

1098
01:40:04.972 --> 01:40:09.182
What I would call a good life.

1099
01:40:09.182 --> 01:40:12.790
I mean, nobody rich or anything.

1100
01:40:12.790 --> 01:40:17.000
But you lived a good life.

1101
01:40:17.000 --> 01:40:26.082
And a lot of it had to do with the school, that we had such good teachers.

1102
01:40:26.082 --> 01:40:34.630
And I know that the teachers . . . Because one I started working with at the school board.

1103
01:40:34.630 --> 01:40:42.644
And I went into one of my coworker's office, and I looked at the picture.

1104
01:40:42.644 --> 01:40:46.384
And I said, "Is that your mother?"

1105
01:40:46.384 --> 01:40:48.521
And she said, "Yeah!"

1106
01:40:48.521 --> 01:40:52.260
I said, "Ms. Calvin was your mother?"

1107
01:40:52.260 --> 01:40:54.932
And so she say, "Yeah!"

1108
01:40:54.932 --> 01:40:56.000
I said,

1109
01:40:56.000 --> 01:40:59.740
<c.p>"Oh, I remember her from . . . at Mossville."

1110
01:40:59.740 --> 01:41:03.479
And she say, "Oh, she loved Mossville.

1111
01:41:03.479 --> 01:41:07.753
She told me that was the best school."

1112
01:41:07.753 --> 01:41:11.493
And I said she taught my nephew.

1113
01:41:11.493 --> 01:41:15.767
She was way behind me when she came.

1114
01:41:15.767 --> 01:41:23.781
But she was a brand new teacher, just out of college, and came and taught.

1115
01:41:23.781 --> 01:41:31.795
Because she lived with my cousin and them, Carolyn Fay Rigmaiden and them down there.

1116
01:41:31.795 --> 01:41:36.603
She lived at they house, and . . . while she worked.

1117
01:41:36.603 --> 01:41:40.342
And I say "Wow, it is amazing."

1118
01:41:40.342 --> 01:41:44.082
And to hear that she loved here.

1119
01:41:44.082 --> 01:41:53.164
That she say, "She tells everybody," she say, "I would get so tired of her saying that,

1120
01:41:53.164 --> 01:41:58.507
that she like . . . that was the best school she taught.”

1121
01:41:58.507 --> 01:42:03.849
And I say, "Wow," I said, "The kids liked her.

1122
01:42:03.849 --> 01:42:08.123
My nephew thought, oh he just loved her."

1123
01:42:08.123 --> 01:42:14.534
So it was just . . . But that's the way that the teachers were.

1124
01:42:14.534 --> 01:42:19.877
Most of them started at Mossville just out of college.

1125
01:42:19.877 --> 01:42:24.151
But then we had the older ones, too.

1126
01:42:24.151 --> 01:42:30.562
But most of them started out of college and they liked it.

1127
01:42:30.562 --> 01:42:33.767
They liked the people around here.

1128
01:42:33.767 --> 01:42:39.110
So I think that tells a lot about the community.

1129
01:42:39.110 --> 01:42:44.986
I was glad to hear that . . . When Monica told me that,

1130
01:42:44.986 --> 01:42:53.000
I was so glad to hear that she said how her mother liked it.

1131
01:42:53.000 --> 01:43:05.252
And so, it's just a good feeling to know that . . . what people that came in thought about the community and the school.

1132
01:43:05.252 --> 01:43:05.809
So.

1133
01:43:05.809 --> 01:43:09.151
I can't say enough about it.

1134
01:43:09.151 --> 01:43:15.277
I don't know why nobody wouldn't have liked to live here.

1135
01:43:15.277 --> 01:43:18.618
And I know some people don't.

1136
01:43:18.618 --> 01:43:23.074
Because my husband didn't want to move here.

1137
01:43:23.074 --> 01:43:25.858
Oh, no. No way [Laughs].

1138
01:43:25.858 --> 01:43:29.200
But it's just . . . Like I say,

1139
01:43:29.200 --> 01:43:41.452
it's just you had to have been here and grown up here to know the value of it, of a little community.

1140
01:43:41.452 --> 01:43:50.363
How great it was, and how great it was to the children that grew up here.

1141
01:43:50.363 --> 01:43:53.148
That taught us a lot.

1142
01:43:53.148 --> 01:43:54.818
We had mentors.

1143
01:43:54.818 --> 01:43:59.274
A majority of the people were our mentors.

1144
01:43:59.274 --> 01:44:03.729
The things that . . . We saw how they lived.

1145
01:44:03.729 --> 01:44:12.640
So you could not go wrong if you followed them, if you saw how they lived.

1146
01:44:12.640 --> 01:44:17.095
It's just no way you could go wrong.

1147
01:44:17.095 --> 01:44:20.437
If you took it at heart.

1148
01:44:20.437 --> 01:44:24.892
And knowing that they done this for you.

1149
01:44:24.892 --> 01:44:32.689
My parents, and all the other parents, they did this for the children coming.

1150
01:44:32.689 --> 01:44:37.702
Wanted us to have better than what they had.

1151
01:44:37.702 --> 01:44:39.929
And so they succeeded.

1152
01:44:39.929 --> 01:44:46.612
They succeeded, and they all should be proud of what they did

1153
01:44:46.612 --> 01:44:49.954
<c.p>because I'm very proud of them.

1154
01:44:49.954 --> 01:44:53.852
That’s the reason I wanted to come,

1155
01:44:53.852 --> 01:45:06.662
and I wanted to come here because I felt like it would be something special for me to come here at this center.

1156
01:45:06.662 --> 01:45:17.243
And so I just . . . that's the reason I wanted to come here instead of at home or somewhere else,

1157
01:45:17.243 --> 01:45:21.142
but it’s just going to be missed.

1158
01:45:21.142 --> 01:45:27.825
And I'm sure it's missed by others now that has already left.

1159
01:45:27.825 --> 01:45:33.951
So it's . . . but it was a beautiful place to grow up.

1160
01:45:33.951 --> 01:45:42.862
I know it's just a little . . . somebody passed through, they wouldn’t even know what it is.

1161
01:45:42.862 --> 01:45:45.089
Just this little place.

1162
01:45:45.089 --> 01:45:46.760
But it's home.

1163
01:45:46.760 --> 01:45:47.874
It's home.

1164
01:45:47.874 --> 01:45:54.000
<v ARSENEAULT>What are you going to miss the most about Mossville?

1165
01:45:54.000 --> 01:46:07.453
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I guess just being able to come here if I want to come and just drive through and be able to tell my children,

1166
01:46:07.453 --> 01:46:15.862
grandchildren, this is where so and so lived, this is where so and so lived.

1167
01:46:15.862 --> 01:46:20.346
We did so and so at this place.

1168
01:46:20.346 --> 01:46:28.194
Because my granddaughter, my youngest granddaughter that's sixteen, she don’t know nothing about Mossville.

1169
01:46:28.194 --> 01:46:32.678
And it hurt me when she said that.

1170
01:46:32.678 --> 01:46:40.526
I did not realize that she hadn't been over here to even see nothing.

1171
01:46:40.526 --> 01:46:47.813
And when I was talking about it, she said, "Well, where is that?"

1172
01:46:47.813 --> 01:46:50.055
And I'm thinking, what?!

1173
01:46:50.055 --> 01:46:59.024
And so it just lets me know how I let it slip with the younger ones.

1174
01:46:59.024 --> 01:47:09.114
And I'm thinking gee, I just didn't realize that she don't know anything, not even anything, about Mossville.

1175
01:47:09.114 --> 01:47:18.083
And so now with all the people so far that's out, I can't really tell her,

1176
01:47:18.083 --> 01:47:25.931
"Okay, this was my cousin, this is cousin, friend, friend,” whatever . . . because they're gone.

1177
01:47:25.931 --> 01:47:31.536
And so I think that that's what I'll miss more,

1178
01:47:31.536 --> 01:47:39.384
is to be able to show my . . . the next generation where I grew up,

1179
01:47:39.384 --> 01:47:48.913
and for them to be able to talk to the people like I could talk to them.

1180
01:47:48.913 --> 01:47:52.837
And a lot of times, I didn't.

1181
01:47:52.837 --> 01:48:01.806
That's why I don't know a lot of things [Laughs] that I should know about Mossville.

1182
01:48:01.806 --> 01:48:16.381
That they will know more and learn and be able to tell somebody else, be able to tell their children, "Oh, this is where my great-

1183
01:48:16.381 --> 01:48:18.062
<c.p>grandmother grew up.”

1184
01:48:18.062 --> 01:48:25.910
So I think that's the part is that all that's going to be gone.

1185
01:48:25.910 --> 01:48:36.000
That that won't be here anymore to pass that part down to our children and grandchildren, great-grandchildren.

1186
01:48:36.000 --> 01:48:41.833
<v ARSENEAULT>So we are at an hour forty-eight.

1187
01:48:41.833 --> 01:48:46.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Oh, wow! [Laughs] Okay.

1188
01:48:46.000 --> 01:48:52.763
<v ARSENEAULT>So I think it would be a good time to stop.

1189
01:48:52.763 --> 01:49:00.141
I wanted to ask you one last question, about Mossville, of course.

1190
01:49:00.141 --> 01:49:07.519
And you've told us already, I'm just going to ask it anyway.

1191
01:49:07.519 --> 01:49:11.822
Like, what does Mossville mean to you?

1192
01:49:11.822 --> 01:49:17.970
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>To me, it was the place where I grew up.

1193
01:49:17.970 --> 01:49:22.274
I was born at Saint Patrick's Hospital.

1194
01:49:22.274 --> 01:49:30.267
I was the only one of the children born outside of the home.

1195
01:49:30.267 --> 01:49:41.333
All the rest of the children were born at home, so they all was born here in Mossville.

1196
01:49:41.333 --> 01:49:45.022
I was the only one not.

1197
01:49:45.022 --> 01:49:54.244
But I have been here . . . I was here all the first years of my life.

1198
01:49:54.244 --> 01:50:01.007
This was a starting point, a learning point, a religious point.

1199
01:50:01.007 --> 01:50:10.230
Anything that you could think of for life, that's what it was here for me.

1200
01:50:10.230 --> 01:50:16.993
It was just a place where you were a close-knit community.

1201
01:50:16.993 --> 01:50:20.681
And you don't find that anymore.

1202
01:50:20.681 --> 01:50:25.600
It's hard to find something like that anymore.

1203
01:50:25.600 --> 01:50:39.741
When I was here, after I got grown, I felt like this was too small to just . . . it wasn't what I wanted anymore.

1204
01:50:39.741 --> 01:50:48.963
But then, once I was away, then I saw that it was what I wanted.

1205
01:50:48.963 --> 01:50:57.570
That this is what I would have loved to have stayed all my life.

1206
01:50:57.570 --> 01:51:02.489
It was a place that people never leave.

1207
01:51:02.489 --> 01:51:12.326
So that makes it, to me, be that type of a community where it inspires you.

1208
01:51:12.326 --> 01:51:16.630
It just . . . it's just in your heart.

1209
01:51:16.630 --> 01:51:21.548
It's in your spirit that this is home.

1210
01:51:21.548 --> 01:51:23.393
Mossville is home.

1211
01:51:23.393 --> 01:51:32.000
Never matter where else I go, or what I do, this is home.

1212
01:51:32.000 --> 01:51:34.074
<v ARSENEAULT>Well thank you so much for sharing.

1213
01:51:34.074 --> 01:51:34.370
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Okay.

1214
01:51:34.370 --> 01:51:37.333
<v ARSENEAULT>And it's an hour fifty-one, so you shared a lot.

1215
01:51:37.333 --> 01:51:37.630
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>Wow.

1216
01:51:37.630 --> 01:51:39.704
And it just don't seem like it.

1217
01:51:39.704 --> 01:51:40.296
<v ARSENEAULT>I know.

1218
01:51:40.296 --> 01:51:42.667
Once you start talking, it's just . . . it's good.

1219
01:51:42.667 --> 01:51:46.815
And is there anything else you want to say before we shut it off?

1220
01:51:46.815 --> 01:51:48.000
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>No, that's it.

1221
01:51:48.000 --> 01:51:48.462
<v ARSENEAULT>You said everything?

1222
01:51:48.462 --> 01:51:48.923
<v RIGMAIDEN-FRANK>I think so.

1223
01:51:48.923 --> 01:51:50.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Alright, well thank you so much.

1224
01:51:50.000 --> 01:51:50.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4508.

1225
01:51:50.000 --> 01:51:50.200
End Session II.]
